('crs_numpy_mastery', 'numpy-mastery', 'NumPy Mastery: The Interactive Course', 'Write, run and debug real NumPy in your browser, from your first array to vectorized machine-learning code.', 'Most people learn NumPy by copying snippets until something works. This course is different: **every lesson is a live coding environment**. You read a short explanation, run the code, change it, break it, and fix it, all inside your browser with a real Python interpreter and NumPy.

## Learn by doing, not by watching

- **Runnable code everywhere.** Every example is editable and runs instantly. Variables persist across cells like a notebook.
- **140 hands-on challenges.** Each one is checked automatically, with progressive hints when you''re stuck.
- **Debug challenges.** You get realistic buggy code (wrong axis, accidental copy, broadcasting mistakes) and fix it.
- **A real debugger.** Step through code line by line, set breakpoints, and watch every variable change.
- **48 predict-the-output questions** build the intuition that separates NumPy beginners from experts.
- **Atlas, your AI tutor,** explains any error in plain language without handing you the answer.

## What you''ll build

You''ll finish with three capstone projects written from scratch in pure NumPy: **k-means clustering**, **linear regression trained with gradient descent**, and a **Game of Life + Monte Carlo simulation**. These are the same building blocks used inside scikit-learn, PyTorch and pandas.

## Why NumPy?

NumPy is the foundation of the entire Python data stack. pandas, scikit-learn, PyTorch, JAX, SciPy and matplotlib all speak its language. Once you think in arrays, everything else gets easier.', 'cat_data-science', 'usr_daniel', 'beginner', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 205, 'published', 1, '["Create, index, reshape and combine N-dimensional arrays with confidence","Replace slow Python loops with fast, vectorized NumPy code","Master broadcasting, the axis argument and views vs. copies","Solve linear systems, compute statistics and work with images as arrays","Debug shape, dtype and broadcasting errors like a pro, with a real step-through debugger","Build k-means, linear regression and simulations from scratch"]', '["Basic Python: variables, lists, loops and functions","A modern browser. Nothing to install; Python runs in the page"]', '["Python developers moving into data science or machine learning","Students who want NumPy fluency before deep learning","Analysts who want to go beyond spreadsheets and pandas one-liners"]', '["NumPy","Python","Interactive","Data Science","Linear Algebra"]', 528, 36, 0, 0, 21360, '2026-02-18T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-01-19T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-04T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_math_for_machine_learning', 'math-for-machine-learning', 'Mathematics for Machine Learning', 'Linear algebra, calculus and probability — explained visually and coded in NumPy.', 'Machine learning is applied mathematics. Every model you train is built from three ideas: **vectors and matrices** represent data, **calculus** tells a model how to improve, and **probability** tells it how confident to be. This course teaches all three the way they are used in practice: geometrically, visually, and in code you run yourself.

## Learn by doing, not by memorizing

- **Runnable code everywhere.** Every formula is backed by a NumPy cell you can edit, run and break.
- **Visual explanations.** Vectors, transformations, eigen-directions, gradient descent and probability distributions are drawn as plots, not only described in words.
- **Challenges with hidden tests.** You write the NumPy yourself and get checked automatically, with hints when you are stuck.
- **Debug challenges.** You fix the bugs machine-learning engineers actually hit: transposed matrices, silent broadcasting, integer truncation and numerical underflow.
- **Predict-the-output questions** that build intuition before you run a single line.

## What you''ll cover

- **Linear algebra:** vectors, dot products and cosine similarity; matrices as transformations; matrix multiplication.
- **Eigenvalues and structure:** eigenvectors, the singular value decomposition (SVD) and principal component analysis (PCA).
- **Calculus and optimization:** derivatives, partial derivatives, gradients, gradient descent and the chain rule behind backpropagation.
- **Probability and statistics:** random variables, expectation, variance and covariance, Bayes'' rule and maximum likelihood estimation.

## Why this course?

Every concept ends with a real machine-learning use. Linear regression is a matrix equation. Dimensionality reduction is an eigenvalue problem. Training a neural network is the chain rule, repeated. A spam filter is Bayes'' rule. Once you can read the math, papers and library documentation stop being opaque.

You need high-school algebra and basic Python. You do not need calculus or statistics before you start.', 'cat_math', 'usr_daniel', 'beginner', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 48, 'published', 1, '["Think geometrically about vectors, matrices and transformations","Use dot products and matrix multiplication the way machine-learning libraries do","Use eigenvalues, the SVD and PCA to find structure in data","Compute gradients and train a model with gradient descent, including the chain rule behind backpropagation","Reason with probability, expectation, Bayes'' rule and maximum likelihood","Read the math in machine-learning papers and turn it into working NumPy code"]', '["High-school algebra: variables, functions and graphs","Basic Python: variables, lists and functions","A modern browser. Nothing to install; Python and NumPy run in the page"]', '["Anyone intimidated by the math behind machine learning","Engineers who want to refresh linear algebra, calculus and probability","Students preparing for a machine-learning course or role"]', '["Linear Algebra","Calculus","Probability","NumPy"]', 292, 20, 0, 0, 39150, '2026-01-26T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-12-27T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-22T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_deep_learning_foundations', 'deep-learning-foundations', 'Deep Learning Foundations', 'Build neural networks from scratch, then master PyTorch — the intuition, the math and the code.', 'Deep learning looks like magic until you have built the machinery yourself. A neural network is a stack of simple functions, its learning is the chain rule applied over and over, and every modern architecture is a small variation on the same loop: predict, measure the error, push the error backwards, take a step. This course builds that loop from first principles and then shows you how the professional tools implement it.

## Learn by building

- **Runnable NumPy everywhere.** Every neuron, layer, gradient and optimizer is code you write, run and break in the page.
- **Gradient checks.** You verify your backpropagation against finite differences, the same technique used to debug real frameworks.
- **Challenges with hidden tests.** Your functions are checked on fresh inputs, so they must be correct, not just pass one example.
- **Debug challenges.** You fix the bugs that real practitioners hit: a missing sum over the batch, a flipped transpose, dropout without rescaling and a learning rate that explodes.
- **PyTorch, mapped to NumPy.** Each PyTorch idea is shown next to the NumPy code you already wrote, so nothing in the framework is magic.

> **Honest note on PyTorch:** the browser runs NumPy, not PyTorch. The PyTorch lessons show complete PyTorch code for you to run on your own machine, and each one has runnable NumPy cells that compute the same thing.

## What you''ll cover

- **Neurons and forward passes:** the artificial neuron, activation functions, batched layers and loss functions.
- **How networks learn:** gradient descent, computational graphs, a tiny autograd engine and backpropagation through a dense layer.
- **Training networks:** full training loops, optimizers, regularization, initialization and normalization.
- **PyTorch in practice:** tensors and autograd, the training loop and a field guide to debugging training.
- **Convolutional and sequence models:** convolutions, CNNs, recurrent networks and the attention mechanism behind Transformers.
- **Capstone:** train and evaluate an image classifier end to end.

## Why this course?

Most introductions show you how to call a framework. This one shows you what the framework is doing. When your model''s loss is NaN, or your validation accuracy is stuck, you will know where to look, because you have already built the parts that can go wrong.

You need Python and basic algebra. Everything else is taught from the ground up.', 'cat_deep-learning', 'usr_maya', 'beginner', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 262, 'published', 1, '["Implement a neural network, its forward pass and backpropagation from scratch in NumPy","Check your gradients numerically and know when a training bug is in the math or the code","Train, regularize and debug multi-layer networks with SGD, momentum and Adam","Write the same training loop in PyTorch, and map every line back to the NumPy version","Build convolutions, CNNs, recurrent networks and attention, and explain how each one works","Finish with a capstone image classifier that you train and evaluate yourself"]', '["Comfortable writing Python functions, loops and lists","High-school algebra. Calculus is taught as you go, with no prior calculus needed","A modern browser. Python and NumPy run in the page; PyTorch code is shown for you to run on your own machine"]', '["Software engineers moving into machine learning","Data analysts who want to understand what happens inside a neural network","Students preparing for machine-learning courses, research or roles"]', '["PyTorch","Neural Networks","Backpropagation","CNNs","Transformers"]', 407, 26, 0, 0, 48210, '2026-01-03T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-12-04T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-09T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_python_for_data_science', 'python-for-data-science', 'Python for Data Science', 'NumPy, pandas, visualization and analysis — the foundation for everything in ML.', 'Data science starts with a simple question and a messy table. This course teaches the everyday craft of turning raw data into answers: writing clear Python, reshaping tables with pandas, cleaning the mistakes that every real dataset contains, drawing charts that tell the truth, and presenting what you found.

## Learn by doing

- **Runnable code everywhere.** Every idea is a cell you can edit, run and break in the page.
- **Real analysis workflows.** You clean a messy sales table, reshape it, and answer business questions with it.
- **Challenges with hidden tests.** Your functions are checked on new data, so a hard-coded answer will not pass.
- **Debug challenges.** You fix the mistakes analysts actually make: chained assignment that silently does nothing, joins that duplicate rows, averages over missing values, and charts with misleading axes.
- **Predict-the-output questions** that build a feel for how pandas behaves before you run it.

## What you''ll cover

- **Python for data:** clean containers, comprehensions and functions you can test.
- **NumPy for data:** arrays, boolean masks and missing values, as the engine under pandas.
- **pandas:** DataFrames, selection, cleaning, grouping, joining, reshaping and time series.
- **Visualization:** matplotlib from first principles, pandas plotting, and the design rules behind honest charts.
- **Exploratory analysis:** a repeatable workflow, outliers, relationships, and a capstone analysis from start to finish.

## Why this course?

Every machine-learning project begins here. Most of the hours in a data job go into loading, checking, cleaning and understanding data, not into fancy models. This course gives you those skills in the order you will use them.

No prior experience is needed. You will get the most from it by running every cell and trying to predict the output first.', 'cat_data-science', 'usr_arjun', 'beginner', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 210, 'published', 0, '["Write clean, idiomatic Python for data work","Select, filter, clean and reshape tabular data with pandas","Group, join and summarize data the way analysts do every day","Work with time series: parse dates, resample and smooth","Plot honest, readable charts with matplotlib and pandas","Run a complete exploratory data analysis and report what you found"]', '["No programming experience required. You will learn Python as you go","A modern browser. Python, NumPy, pandas and matplotlib run in the page"]', '["Complete beginners who want a career in data","Analysts moving from spreadsheets to code","Engineers who need to explore data before building models"]', '["Python","pandas","NumPy","Free"]', 306, 19, 0, 0, 88420, '2025-12-11T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-11-11T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-14T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_python_basics', 'python-basics', 'Python Basics: The Interactive Course', 'Go from your first line of code to real programs, writing, running and debugging Python right in your browser.', 'Most beginner courses have you watch someone else type. This one puts the keyboard in your hands from the first minute: **every lesson is a live coding environment** with a real Python interpreter running inside your browser.

## Learn by doing, not by watching

- **No videos, no installs.** Read a short explanation, run the code, change it, break it and fix it, right on the page.
- **Over 100 hands-on challenges.** Each one is checked automatically by hidden tests, with progressive hints when you''re stuck.
- **Debug challenges.** Real beginner bugs (off-by-one loops, shared lists, forgotten `return`s) that you track down and fix.
- **A real debugger.** Step through your code line by line, *backwards and forwards*, and watch every variable change.
- **Predict-the-output questions** train you to read code like an expert before you run it.
- **Atlas, your AI tutor,** explains any error in plain language without handing you the answer.

## From zero to real programs

You''ll start with `print("Hello")` and finish by building three complete projects: a **gradebook analyzer** that reads and reports on CSV data, a **word-guessing game** with scoring and replay, and a **budget tracker** built with classes that saves to JSON.

## Built for what comes next

Everything here is the foundation for data science, automation, web development and machine learning. When you''re done, **Python Advanced** picks up exactly where this course ends.', 'cat_programming', 'usr_arjun', 'beginner', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 45, 'published', 1, '["Write clean, working Python programs from a blank page, with no setup or installs","Master variables, strings, numbers, conditions and loops until they feel automatic","Organize data with lists, tuples, dictionaries and sets, and know which one to reach for","Write reusable functions, handle errors gracefully and work with files, CSV and JSON","Read tracebacks and fix bugs with a real step-through debugger instead of guessing","Model real things with classes, and build three complete projects from scratch"]', '["No programming experience at all. We start from the very first line","A modern browser. Python runs inside the page, so there''s nothing to install"]', '["Complete beginners who want to learn to code properly, not just copy snippets","Analysts, marketers and scientists who want to automate their work with Python","Students preparing for data science, machine learning or a CS degree","Developers from other languages who want a fast, thorough tour of Python"]', '["Python","Interactive","Programming","Beginner","Fundamentals"]', 587, 38, 0, 0, 34870, '2025-11-18T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-10-19T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-17T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_javascript_basics', 'javascript-basics', 'JavaScript Basics: The Interactive Course', 'Go from your first console.log to real programs. Write, run and debug JavaScript in your browser, with no setup.', 'Most beginner courses have you watch someone else type. This one puts the keyboard in your hands from the first minute: **every lesson is a live coding environment** with a real JavaScript engine running inside your browser.

## Learn by doing, not by watching

- **No videos, no installs.** Read a short explanation, run the code, change it, break it and fix it, right on the page.
- **Hands-on challenges in every lesson.** Each one is checked by hidden tests that use new inputs, so you have to understand the idea, not memorize an answer. Progressive hints appear when you''re stuck.
- **Debug challenges.** Real beginner bugs (`==` surprises, off-by-one loops, shared arrays, a forgotten `return`) that you track down and fix.
- **Predict-the-output questions** train you to read code like an experienced developer before you run it.
- **Atlas, your AI tutor,** explains any error in plain language without handing you the answer.

## From zero to real programs

You''ll start with `console.log` and finish by building three complete projects: a **grade report** that summarizes student data, a **to-do manager** built with objects and arrays, and a **budget tracker** built with classes that saves to JSON.

## Built for what comes next

Everything here is the foundation for front-end development, Node.js and full-stack work. When you''re done, **JavaScript Advanced** picks up exactly where this course ends, with closures, the event loop, promises and async patterns.', 'cat_programming', 'usr_arjun', 'beginner', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 60, 'published', 1, '["Write clear JavaScript from a blank page, with nothing to install","Store and change data with let, const, numbers, strings and booleans","Control program flow with conditionals, loops and the array methods real code uses","Write functions, understand scope, and transform data with map, filter and reduce","Model things with objects and classes, and read and write JSON","Handle errors with try/catch, work with dates, and build three complete projects"]', '["No programming experience needed. We start from console.log(\"Hello\")","A modern browser. JavaScript runs inside the page, so there''s nothing to install"]', '["Complete beginners who want to build for the web, starting with the language itself","Designers and marketers who want to script, automate and prototype","Developers moving from Python, Java or C# who need the JavaScript mental model fast","Anyone preparing for React, Node.js or front-end interviews"]', '["JavaScript","Interactive","Programming","Beginner","Web"]', 513, 32, 0, 0, 21460, '2025-10-26T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-09-26T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-05T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_javascript_advanced', 'javascript-advanced', 'JavaScript Advanced: Closures, Async & the Event Loop', 'Master the parts of JavaScript that separate working code from reliable code: scope, prototypes, async and the event loop.', 'Most developers learn JavaScript by trial and error. This course replaces the guessing with a precise mental model, and every idea is something you **run, predict and break** on the page.

## Understand the machine

- **Execution, scope and closures** with exact rules you can predict, not folklore.
- **`this`, prototypes and classes** explained from the bottom up.
- **The event loop**, step by step: why a resolved promise can run before a zero-second timer.

## Write async code you can trust

- **Promises and async/await** with error handling that doesn''t swallow failures.
- **Concurrency patterns**: `Promise.all`, `allSettled`, `race`, and worker pools with a concurrency limit.
- **Cancellation, retries and caching** that behave under load.

## Build real things

Three capstone projects: an **async task queue**, a **reactive store** with subscriptions (the core idea behind modern state libraries), and an **API client** with retries, caching and cancellation.

Every challenge is checked by hidden tests, and **Atlas**, the AI tutor, is ready for the moments when the event loop refuses to make sense.', 'cat_programming', 'usr_lena', 'advanced', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 200, 'published', 0, '["Explain how JavaScript runs code: the call stack, scope, hoisting and closures","Control this, prototypes and classes instead of guessing at them","Write generators, iterators and proxies, and know when a language feature earns its place","Use promises and async/await correctly, including ordering, cancellation and error handling","Predict the event loop: microtasks versus macrotasks, and why callbacks run when they do","Build production patterns: concurrency limits, reactive stores, retries and caching"]', '["Solid JavaScript basics: variables, functions, arrays, objects and loops","Our JavaScript Basics course, or equivalent experience"]', '["Developers who write JavaScript daily but want to stop guessing how it behaves","Front-end and Node.js engineers preparing for senior interviews","Engineers debugging async bugs, race conditions or memory leaks","Anyone who wants to read framework source code, from React to Express, with confidence"]', '["JavaScript","Interactive","Async","Advanced","Programming"]', 653, 31, 0, 0, 8745, '2025-10-03T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-09-03T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-11T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_java_basics', 'java-basics', 'Java Basics: The Interactive Course', 'Go from your first line of code to real programs, writing, running and debugging Java right in your browser.', 'Most beginner courses have you watch someone else type. This one puts the keyboard in your hands from the first minute: **every lesson is a live coding environment** with a real Java compiler and JVM running inside your browser.

## Learn by doing, not by watching

- **No videos, no installs.** Read a short explanation, run the code, change it, break it and fix it, right on the page.
- **Over 100 hands-on challenges.** Each one is checked by hidden tests, with progressive hints when you''re stuck.
- **Debug challenges.** Real beginner bugs (off-by-one loops, `=` instead of `==`, integer division, null references) that you track down and fix.
- **Predict-the-output questions** train you to read code like an experienced developer before you run it.
- **Atlas, your AI tutor,** explains any compiler error or exception in plain language without handing you the answer.

## From zero to real programs

You''ll start with `System.out.println("Hello")` and finish by building three complete projects: a **grade report** that summarizes student data, a **to-do manager** built with objects and lists, and a **bank account** built with classes, encapsulation and exceptions.

## Built for what comes next

Everything here is the foundation for Android, Spring, backend services and a CS degree. Java''s static types, collections and object model show up in almost every codebase you will meet.', 'cat_programming', 'usr_arjun', 'beginner', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 25, 'published', 1, '["Write and run Java from a blank page, with nothing to install","Master variables, types, strings, conditionals, loops and arrays","Organize data with ArrayList, HashMap and HashSet, and sort it with confidence","Write methods and classes that model real things, and understand inheritance and interfaces","Read compiler errors and stack traces, and fix bugs instead of guessing","Build three complete projects: a grade report, a to-do list and a bank account"]', '["No programming experience at all. We start from the very first line","A modern browser. Java and its compiler run inside the page, so there''s nothing to install"]', '["Complete beginners who want a thorough, practical first language","Students preparing for a CS course, AP Computer Science or a coding interview","Developers from Python, JavaScript or other languages who need Java''s static types and object model","Anyone who wants to build Android apps, backend services or enterprise software later"]', '["Java","Interactive","Programming","Beginner","Object-Oriented"]', 591, 38, 0, 0, 18320, '2025-09-10T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-08-11T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-04T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_c_basics', 'c-basics', 'C Basics: The Interactive Course', 'Learn C from your first printf to pointers, memory and structs. Predict what real C code does before you compile it.', 'C is the language underneath almost everything: operating systems, databases, game engines and the firmware in the devices around you. It is small, fast and honest. It also expects you to understand what the machine is doing. This course teaches that understanding from the first line.

## Predict first, then compile

Every lesson is interactive. Most of its examples are **predict-the-output questions**: you read a short C program, choose what it prints, and only then see the explanation. Reasoning about code before you run it is the habit that separates careful C programmers from frustrated ones.

- **Real C, not pseudo-code.** Every example is a complete, standard C program you can compile unchanged.
- **Explanations that teach the rule.** Each answer explains the underlying behavior, such as integer division, operator precedence or what `sizeof` measures, so the same mistake is hard to repeat.
- **Checkpoint quizzes** after each section, with a written explanation for every answer.
- **Three capstone projects** built step by step: a grade report, a contact book that saves to a file, and a word-statistics tool.

## Run it on your own machine

The lessons are written so you can compile and run every program with gcc or clang. Lesson 01 shows how to install a compiler on Windows, macOS or Linux in a few minutes. Your browser shows the code and checks your reasoning. Your terminal proves it.

## From the first printf to real memory

You will start with variables, types and `printf`, then move through decisions, loops, functions and arrays. The middle of the course is pointers, the idea that unlocks C. You finish with structs, the preprocessor and file I/O, and you leave able to read most C code you come across.

## Built for what comes next

This course is the foundation for **C Advanced**, which covers dynamic data structures, undefined behavior, sanitizers and secure coding, and for any systems course that follows.', 'cat_programming', 'usr_arjun', 'beginner', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 25, 'published', 1, '["Write, compile and run small C programs with gcc or clang, and read compiler errors with confidence","Choose the right types, control integer and floating-point behavior, and write clear expressions","Control program flow with if, switch and loops, and structure code with functions and scope","Work with arrays and strings the way C does, including the null terminator and the standard string functions","Understand pointers, pointer arithmetic and dynamic memory, the features that make C both powerful and unforgiving","Model data with structs, enums and unions, split code with the preprocessor, and read and write files safely"]', '["No programming experience needed. We start from a blank file and build up","A C compiler on your computer for the optional run-it-yourself steps: gcc or clang. Lesson 01 shows how to install one"]', '["Complete beginners who want to learn the language that most operating systems, databases and embedded devices are written in","Students preparing for systems programming, operating systems or computer architecture courses","Programmers moving from Python, Java or JavaScript who need to understand memory and types","Engineers who work near hardware and want to read C code confidently"]', '["C","Interactive","Programming","Beginner","Systems"]', 506, 30, 0, 0, 0, '2025-08-18T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-07-19T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-05T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_c_advanced', 'c-advanced', 'C Advanced: Memory, Data Structures and Systems Craft', 'Take full control of C: manage memory safely, build real data structures, and write code you can trust.', 'C rewards the programmers who understand it deeply and punishes everyone else. **C Advanced** is for the people who have learned the syntax and now want the judgment: how the compiler really reads your code, who owns each block of memory, and why a program that "works" on your laptop can still be broken.

## Think like a systems programmer

- **Start from the toolchain.** You will follow a file through preprocessing, compilation, assembly and linking, and learn to read what each stage produces.
- **Master memory.** Ownership rules, leak detection, realloc pitfalls and the discipline that keeps large programs alive.
- **Build the classics yourself.** Linked lists, stacks, queues, binary search trees and hash tables, each implemented with clear invariants and tested edge cases.
- **Write generic, portable code.** Function pointers, comparators, void-pointer containers, X-macros and opaque types, the tools real C libraries are built with.
- **Debug like a professional.** gdb, AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and Valgrind, used on purpose rather than as a last resort.

## Every lesson is interactive

There are no videos. Each lesson is a guided walkthrough with predict-the-output questions that make you reason about memory, pointers and control flow before you see the answer, plus checkpoint quizzes that test the ideas you need for the projects.

## Finish with real projects

Three capstone projects bring everything together: a **generic dynamic array library**, a **key-value store** with a hash table and file persistence, and a **tokenizer and expression evaluator** that could sit inside a calculator or a configuration language.', 'cat_programming', 'usr_arjun', 'advanced', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 60, 'published', 0, '["Explain what the compiler does with your code, from preprocessing to linking, and why undefined behavior is dangerous","Design ownership rules for heap memory and find leaks and invalid accesses before they reach production","Build linked lists, stacks, queues, binary search trees and hash tables from first principles","Sort any data with qsort, write comparators, and use callbacks and function pointers for flexible APIs","Work with bits, struct layout, unions and type punning with confidence and portable code","Write generic containers with void pointers, handle errors with errno, and debug with gdb and sanitizers","Finish three capstone projects: a generic dynamic array, a persistent key-value store and an expression evaluator"]', '["Complete C Basics, or be comfortable with variables, control flow, functions, arrays, pointers and structs","A C11 compiler such as GCC or Clang on your own machine, for the exercises you choose to run locally"]', '["Programmers who know C syntax and want to write safe, efficient and maintainable systems code","Engineers moving to embedded, operating systems, databases or game engines","Students preparing for systems programming courses and technical interviews","Developers who maintain legacy C and want to understand the memory and undefined behavior traps they inherit"]', '["C","Interactive","Systems","Data Structures","Memory","Advanced"]', 629, 29, 0, 0, 0, '2025-07-26T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-06-26T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-22T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_html_css_basics', 'html-css-basics', 'HTML & CSS Basics: The Interactive Course', 'Build real, beautiful web pages from your first tag. Write HTML and CSS in the browser and watch every change render instantly.', 'Most beginner web courses show you a finished page and ask you to copy it. This one gives you a **live browser preview under every example**, so you can change a line and see the consequence immediately.

## Learn by looking, changing and breaking

- **Every lesson is a live playground.** Write HTML and CSS, run it, and see the real page rendered by your browser. No setup, no build step, no guessing.
- **Dozens of hands-on challenges.** Each one is checked against the real DOM and real computed styles, not a screenshot or a multiple-choice quiz. Progressive hints appear when you are stuck.
- **Debug challenges.** Real beginner bugs — a missing closing tag, a broken selector, a margin that collapses, an image without `alt` — that you track down and fix.
- **Predict-the-render questions** train you to read CSS before you run it, which is the skill that separates people who guess from people who know.
- **A responsive preview toolbar.** Check every page at desktop, tablet and phone width without leaving the lesson.

## From a blank file to a real page

You start with `<h1>Hello, web</h1>` and finish with two portfolio-ready projects: a **personal profile page** with a responsive layout and a **product landing page** with a hero, feature grid, pricing cards and an accessible signup form.

## Built for what comes next

Everything here is the foundation for front-end development, design systems and JavaScript. When you are done, **HTML & CSS Advanced** picks up with the cascade, container queries, modern selectors, motion and production accessibility.', 'cat_web-development', 'usr_noah', 'beginner', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 198, 'published', 1, '["Structure pages with semantic HTML that browsers, search engines and screen readers understand","Style with selectors, the box model, typography and color until a page looks intentional","Build layouts with Flexbox and CSS Grid instead of fighting floats and margins","Make pages responsive with mobile-first media queries and fluid type","Create accessible links, images, forms and focus states from the start","Ship two complete projects: a personal profile page and a product landing page"]', '["No coding experience at all. We start with your first tag","A modern browser. Pages render live on the page, so there is nothing to install"]', '["Complete beginners who want to build for the web and understand what they are typing","Designers who want to turn mockups into working, responsive pages","Marketers and founders who need to edit a landing page without fear","Developers from other languages who want a precise, modern HTML and CSS foundation"]', '["HTML","CSS","Web Design","Frontend","Responsive","Beginner"]', 396, 23, 0, 0, 24680, '2025-07-03T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-06-03T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-11T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_html_css_advanced', 'html-css-advanced', 'HTML & CSS Advanced: Systems, Motion & Modern Layout', 'Master the cascade, build resilient layout systems and ship accessible, animated interfaces that hold up in production.', 'Beginner CSS teaches you properties. Advanced CSS teaches you the **system underneath them**: the cascade, formatting contexts, intrinsic sizing, containment and the tiny set of rules that explain a thousand layout bugs.

## Go from "it works on my screen" to "it works everywhere"

- **Real computed styles, real rendering.** Every challenge runs in the browser and is checked against the DOM, so you learn what the engine actually does.
- **The cascade, demystified.** Specificity, inheritance, `@layer`, `:where()` and `!important` — with a decision framework you can use at work.
- **Layout that survives content.** Flexbox and Grid deep dives, including the min-content traps, `minmax()`, `auto-fit` and container queries.
- **Motion with judgement.** Transforms, keyframes, view transitions, timing and reduced-motion support.
- **Accessibility and performance as engineering.** Contrast, focus management, ARIA rules, responsive images, font loading and layout shift.

## Two production-grade capstones

Build a **responsive analytics dashboard** with a sidebar, fluid cards and accessible data tables, then extract the visual language into a **reusable design system** with tokens, components and a dark theme. Both projects are checked by the same DOM assertions used throughout the course.

## Who this is for

If you can make a page but sometimes feel like you are guessing at `z-index`, `min-width: auto`, or why a grid track refuses to shrink, this course is for you.', 'cat_web-development', 'usr_noah', 'advanced', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, NULL, 265, 'published', 0, '["Reason about the cascade, specificity, inheritance and modern cascade layers instead of fighting them","Build themeable interfaces with custom properties, design tokens and dark mode","Use :is(), :where(), :has() and container queries to write smaller, more resilient CSS","Master Flexbox and Grid, including minmax(), auto-fit, named areas and intrinsic sizing","Create motion that feels expensive and respects prefers-reduced-motion","Audit and fix accessibility, focus order, contrast and performance on real pages","Ship a responsive analytics dashboard and a reusable design system"]', '["Comfortable with HTML elements and basic CSS selectors","Our HTML & CSS Basics course, or equivalent experience","A modern browser (Chrome, Safari, Firefox or Edge)"]', '["Front-end developers who can build a page but want to understand why it breaks","Designers moving into design engineering and component work","Full-stack engineers who want to ship polished, accessible interfaces","Anyone preparing for senior front-end interviews or design-system work"]', '["CSS Architecture","Grid","Container Queries","Accessibility","Animation","Design Systems"]', 455, 22, 0, 0, 9860, '2025-06-10T06:40:00.179Z', '2025-05-11T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-14T06:40:00.179Z'),
('crs_draft_multimodal', 'multimodal-ai-systems', 'Multimodal AI Systems', 'Combine vision, audio and language models into a single product experience.', 'Prompting, RAG, fine-tuning, evaluation and deployment for real-world LLM applications.

## About this course

**LLM Engineering: From Prompt to Production** is a hands-on program designed and taught by practitioners. Every module pairs a clear explanation with code you write yourself, so you leave with skills — not just notes.

### How it works

- **Short, focused lessons** with downloadable notebooks
- **Checkpoints and quizzes** to lock in understanding
- **An AI teaching assistant** that knows the course material and answers questions 24/7
- **A verifiable certificate** when you complete every lesson

### Why learn with us

The field moves quickly, so we update our courses continuously as tools and best practices evolve. You get lifetime access to every update.', 'cat_generative-ai', 'usr_sofia', 'intermediate', 'English', 0, NULL, 'USD', NULL, 'https://commondatastorage.googleapis.com/gtv-videos-bucket/sample/ForBiggerBlazes.mp4', 185, 'draft', 0, '["Design prompts and structured outputs that are reliable at scale","Build retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with vector search","Fine-tune open models with LoRA for your domain","Evaluate LLM systems with automated and human-in-the-loop evals","Control cost and latency with caching, routing and batching","Deploy, monitor and guardrail LLM features in production"]', '["Solid Python","Basic familiarity with APIs and JSON","No prior ML experience required"]', '["Software engineers building AI features","Tech leads evaluating LLM architectures","Founders prototyping AI products"]', '["Multimodal","Vision-Language","Audio"]', 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, '2026-08-14T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-03T06:40:00.179Z');

