the first item, and the starred name collects everything else **as a list**, so `rest` is `[20, 30, 40]`. The star is what prevents the ''too many values'' error."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"Starting from `tally = {}`, what is `tally` after running `for ch in \"banana\": tally[ch] = tally.get(ch, 0) + 1`?","options":["`{''a'': 3, ''b'': 1, ''n'': 2}`","`KeyError: ''b''`","`{''b'': 1, ''a'': 3, ''n'': 2}`","`{''b'': 1, ''a'': 1, ''n'': 1}`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`.get(ch, 0)` returns 0 for a letter that hasn''t been seen yet, so there''s no `KeyError`, and each letter''s count goes up by 1 every time it appears. Dictionaries keep insertion order, so the keys appear in the order first seen: b, a, n."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"With `roster = {\"Ana\": 7, \"Ben\": 10}`, which expression is `True`?","options":["`\"Ana\" in roster`","`7 in roster`","`(\"Ana\", 7) in roster`","`10 in roster.keys()`"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"`in` on a dictionary checks its **keys**, and the keys here are the names. The jersey numbers are values, so you''d need `7 in roster.values()` to search them."},{"id":"q8","prompt":"You write `seen = {}` and later call `seen.add(\"Maya\")`. What happens?","options":["`\"Maya\"` is added to an empty set","`\"Maya\"` is added as a dictionary key with the value `None`","Nothing; `.add()` on an empty collection is ignored","`AttributeError`, because `{}` is an empty dictionary, which has no `.add()` method"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Curly braces were used for dictionaries first, so `{}` is an empty **dict**. Dictionaries don''t have `.add()`. An empty set must be written `set()`."},{"id":"q9","prompt":"What does `sorted({1, 2, 3} ^ {2, 3, 4})` return?","options":["`[2, 3]`","`[1, 2, 3, 4]`","`[1, 4]`","`[1]`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`^` is the symmetric difference: items in exactly one of the two sets. 2 and 3 are in both, so they''re left out, leaving 1 and 4. (`&` would give `[2, 3]` and `|` would give `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.)"},{"id":"q10","prompt":"With `nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]`, which comprehension produces `[0, 2, 0, 4]`?","options":["`[n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0]`","`[n if n % 2 == 0 else 0 for n in nums]`","`[0 if n % 2 == 0 else n for n in nums]`","`[n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0 else 0]`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To keep every position and **choose** a value for each item, `if ... else` goes at the front. An `if` at the end **filters** (giving `[2, 4]`), and an `else` at the end is a `SyntaxError`. The third option swaps the values, giving `[1, 0, 3, 0]`."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z');

