('les_python_basics_7_3', 'sec_python_basics_7', 'crs_python_basics', 'Checkpoint: objects & classes', 'quiz', 2, 420, NULL, NULL, 'Answer all questions — you need **70%** to pass. You can retake the quiz as many times as you like.', 0, '[{"id":"q1","prompt":"Given `class Pet:` with a method `def describe(self): ...`, what does `rex.describe()` actually do?","options":["Calls `Pet.describe(rex)`, so inside the method `self` is `rex`","Calls `describe()` with no arguments at all","Creates a new Pet and calls `describe` on it","Calls `Pet.describe(Pet)`, so `self` is the class"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Calling a method on an object passes that object in as the first argument. `self` is just the name of the parameter that receives it: \"this particular object\"."},{"id":"q2","prompt":"A `Counter` class sets `self.count = 0` in `__init__`, and its method is `def add(self): count = self.count + 1`. After `c = Counter()`, `c.add()`, `c.add()`, what does `print(c.count)` show?","options":["2","1","An AttributeError","0"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"`count = self.count + 1` creates a local variable that vanishes when `add` returns. The object''s attribute is never updated. The fix is `self.count += 1`."},{"id":"q3","prompt":"Calling `rex.speak()` raises `TypeError: Pet.speak() takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given`. What''s the most likely bug?","options":["`rex` was never created","The method was defined as `def speak():` without `self`","`speak` needs to return a string","You must call it as `Pet.speak()` instead"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`rex.speak()` silently passes `rex` as the first argument. If the method has no `self` parameter, there''s nowhere to put it, hence \"1 was given\"."},{"id":"q4","prompt":"A class has `tricks = []` written directly in the class body, and a method does `self.tricks.append(trick)`. What happens when two different dogs each learn a trick?","options":["Each dog gets its own list automatically","Python raises an error because class attributes can''t be changed","Both tricks end up in one list shared by every dog","Only the first dog''s trick is saved"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A class attribute is one object shared by all instances, and `.append()` changes that shared list. Lists and dicts that each object should own must be created in `__init__` with `self.tricks = []`."},{"id":"q5","prompt":"A class is defined as `class Account: rate = 0.02`. After `a = Account()`, `b = Account()` and `a.rate = 0.05`, what does `print(b.rate)` show?","options":["0.05","0.02","An AttributeError","None"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Assigning `a.rate = 0.05` creates a new attribute on `a` only. `b` has no `rate` of its own, so reading `b.rate` falls back to the class value, `0.02`."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"A class defines both `__str__` and `__repr__`. Which one does `print([item])` use for the object inside the list?","options":["`__str__`, because `print` always uses `__str__`","Neither; lists print memory addresses","`__init__`","`__repr__`, because containers show their items with `__repr__`"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"`print(item)` would use `__str__`, but a list builds its text from each item''s `__repr__`. That''s why `__repr__` is the one to write if you only write one."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"Printing an object crashes with `TypeError: __str__ returned non-string (type NoneType)`. Which fix is right?","options":["Change `print(...)` inside `__str__` to `return ...`","Wrap the call as `print(str(obj))`","Rename `__str__` to `__repr__`","Add `self` to the `print` call inside `__str__`"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"`__str__` must return a string. A `__str__` that prints its text and returns nothing hands `None` back to Python, which raises this TypeError."},{"id":"q8","prompt":"In `class Rectangle`, `area` is defined with `@property`. How do you read a rectangle''s area?","options":["`room.area()`","`room.area`","`Rectangle.area()`","`property(room.area)`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A property is read like an attribute, without parentheses. `room.area()` would compute the number and then try to call it, raising `TypeError: ''int'' object is not callable`."},{"id":"q9","prompt":"`class SavingsAccount(Account)` defines its own `__init__` that only sets `self.rate = rate`. Creating one and reading `.balance` raises AttributeError. What''s missing?","options":["`SavingsAccount` must also define `deposit`","A `@property` for balance","A call to `super().__init__(owner, balance)` inside the child''s `__init__`","The parent class name should be in quotes"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"When a child defines `__init__`, the parent''s `__init__` no longer runs automatically. Calling `super().__init__(...)` lets `Account` set up `owner` and `balance` before the child adds `rate`."},{"id":"q10","prompt":"Which design uses composition (\"has a\") rather than inheritance (\"is a\")?","options":["`class Manager(Employee)`","`class Puppy(Dog)`","`class SavingsAccount(Account)`","A `Playlist` class whose `songs` attribute holds a list of `Song` objects"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"A playlist is not a kind of song; it has songs, so it stores them as an attribute. The other three are genuine \"is a\" relationships, where inheritance fits."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z');

