('les_javascript_advanced_1_5', 'sec_javascript_advanced_1', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'Checkpoint: how JavaScript runs', 'quiz', 4, 420, NULL, NULL, 'Answer all questions — you need **70%** to pass. You can retake the quiz as many times as you like.', 0, '[{"id":"q1","prompt":"What does `run()` print?\n\n```js\nfunction run() {\n  console.log(typeof later);\n  var later = 1;\n}\nrun();\n```","options":["number","undefined","ReferenceError","object"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`var` is hoisted to the top of its function as a name, with the value `undefined`. The assignment `later = 1` runs only when execution reaches that line, so `typeof later` reads `undefined` on the first line. The tempting `ReferenceError` belongs to `let` and `const`, not `var`."},{"id":"q2","prompt":"What does `run()` print?\n\n```js\nfunction run() {\n  try {\n    console.log(value);\n  } catch (e) {\n    console.log(e.name);\n  }\n  let value = 1;\n}\nrun();\n```","options":["undefined","1","ReferenceError","null"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A `let` binding is hoisted but uninitialized until its declaration runs. Reading it inside the temporal dead zone throws a `ReferenceError`. A `var` in the same position would print `undefined`, which is why `let` makes ordering mistakes fail loudly."},{"id":"q3","prompt":"A function calls itself with no base case. Which error does the engine throw when the call stack runs out of room?","options":["RangeError","TypeError","SyntaxError","StackOverflowError"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Engines throw a `RangeError` when the call stack exceeds its limit. `TypeError` means a value had the wrong type, and `StackOverflowError` is a Java name that JavaScript does not use. The limit depends on the engine, so the code should loop instead of recursing on unbounded input."},{"id":"q4","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nconsole.log(square(4));\nfunction square(n) {\n  return n * n;\n}\n```","options":["undefined","ReferenceError","NaN","16"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"A function declaration is hoisted together with its body, so `square` is callable before the line where it appears. An arrow function or function expression assigned to a `const` would not be, and calling it early would throw a `ReferenceError` instead."},{"id":"q5","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nconst fns = [];\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n  fns.push(() => i);\n}\nconsole.log(fns.map((f) => f()).join(\" \"));\n```","options":["0 1 2","3 3 3","undefined undefined undefined","2 2 2"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"All three arrow functions close over the single `var i` of the enclosing function. When they run after the loop, `i` is `3`. Changing the loop to `let i` gives each iteration its own binding, and the output becomes `0 1 2`. The option `0 1 2` is the answer you get with `let`, and `2 2 2` is what you''d see by mistaking the loop''s last index for its final value."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nfunction show() {\n  return this.v;\n}\nconst bound = show.bind({ v: 1 });\nconsole.log(bound.call({ v: 2 }));\n```","options":["1","2","undefined","TypeError"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"`bind` permanently fixes the receiver of a function. A later `call` with a different receiver has no effect, so `this.v` still reads `1`. Explicit binding made by `bind` takes priority over the receiver passed to `call`."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nif (false) {\n  var secret = \"hi\";\n}\nconsole.log(secret);\n```","options":["hi","ReferenceError","undefined","null"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`var` is scoped to the whole function (here, the top level), not to the `if` block. The declaration is hoisted, and since the block never runs, `secret` exists with the value `undefined`. Had it been `let secret`, the name would be scoped to the block and reading it outside would throw a `ReferenceError`."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z');

