('les_javascript_advanced_2_1', 'sec_javascript_advanced_2', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'Prototypes and the prototype chain', 'interactive', 0, 1440, NULL, NULL, 'Every object in JavaScript has a hidden link to another object, its prototype. When you read a property and the object doesn''t have it, the engine doesn''t give up. It follows the link and checks there, then follows the next link, and so on until it finds the property or runs out of links. That chain of links is the prototype chain, and it''s the reason methods like `push` and `toUpperCase` work on values that never defined them.

Most developers meet prototypes through error messages and a little bit of `class` syntax, and they treat the chain as magic. This lesson makes it concrete. You''ll see exactly where a lookup goes, why assignment behaves differently from reading, and the shared-state bugs that come from putting mutable data on a prototype. Those bugs are subtle enough to survive code review, which is why they''re worth understanding in detail.

## Lookup walks the chain

Start with a plain link. `Object.create(proto)` creates a new object whose prototype is `proto`. Reading a property on the new object checks its own properties first, and then the prototype.

```js run
const animal = {
  legs: 4,
  breathe() {
    return "breathing";
  },
};

const dog = Object.create(animal);
dog.bark = function () {
  return "woof";
};

console.log(dog.legs, dog.breathe(), dog.bark());
console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(dog) === animal, Object.hasOwn(dog, "legs"));
```

`dog.legs` isn''t an own property of `dog`, so the engine reads it from `animal`. `dog.breathe` is found one link up. `dog.bark` is an own property. The last line separates the two questions that matter: is `animal` the prototype of `dog`, and does `dog` own `legs`? `Object.hasOwn` answers the second one, and it''s the safe way to check ownership.

> **Under the hood:** The prototype is an internal slot called `[[Prototype]]`. It isn''t a normal property you can see with `Object.keys`. `Object.getPrototypeOf` reads it, and `Object.setPrototypeOf` changes it, although changing it after an object is created is slow and should be avoided.

## Reading and writing follow different rules

Reading a property walks the chain. Writing a property does not. When you assign `child.count = 5`, the engine creates or updates an **own** property on `child`, and it leaves the prototype alone. This is called shadowing: the own property hides the inherited one until you delete it.

```js run
const base = { level: 1 };
const child = Object.create(base);

child.level = 5;
console.log(base.level, child.level);

delete child.level;
console.log(child.level);
```

After the assignment, `child` has its own `level`, and the prototype''s `level` is unchanged. Deleting the own property reveals the inherited value again. Shadowing is how JavaScript lets an instance override a default without touching the defaults for everyone else.

There''s one exception worth knowing. If the prototype has a setter for that name, assignment calls the setter instead of creating an own property. That''s how `class` setters work, and you''ll see it in lesson 6.

## Constructors and `prototype`

Functions have a `prototype` property. When you call a function with `new`, the new object''s prototype is set to that function''s `prototype`. That single fact is how constructor functions share methods across every instance.

```js run
function Robot(name) {
  this.name = name;
}

Robot.prototype.greet = function () {
  return "I am " + this.name;
};

const r1 = new Robot("R1");
const r2 = new Robot("R2");

console.log(r1.greet(), r2.greet(), r1.greet === r2.greet);
console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(r1) === Robot.prototype);
```

`name` is set on each instance, because each robot has its own name. `greet` lives once on `Robot.prototype`, and every instance reaches it through the chain. `r1.greet === r2.greet` is `true` because there''s only one function. This is why methods belong on the prototype rather than being recreated in the constructor for every object.

> **In the wild:** The DOM is one long prototype chain. A `<button>` element inherits from `HTMLButtonElement`, then `HTMLElement`, `Element`, `Node` and `EventTarget`. When you call `addEventListener` on a button, the method is found three or four links up, and nothing about the button had to define it.

> **Pitfall:** `Robot.prototype` is not the same thing as `Object.getPrototypeOf(robot)`, although they''re the same object for instances. The `prototype` property belongs to the function, and it''s the value used when the function is called with `new`. The `[[Prototype]]` link belongs to each object.

## The shared-state trap

Because lookup goes through the prototype, a mutable value on a prototype is shared by every object that inherits from it. Reading it is fine. Mutating it through an instance changes it for all instances, and the instance never gets its own copy.

```js run
const proto = { tags: [] };
const a = Object.create(proto);
const b = Object.create(proto);

a.tags.push("x");
console.log(b.tags, proto.tags === a.tags);
```

`a.tags.push("x")` reads `tags` through the chain, gets the shared array, and pushes into it. Nothing was assigned to `a.tags`, so no own property was created. Every object that inherits from `proto` now sees the change.

> **Pitfall:** Never put arrays or plain objects on a prototype, and never rely on a constructor''s `prototype` for per-instance state. Assign them in the constructor, where each instance gets its own. The same rule applies to any object that a library puts on a prototype and asks you to mutate.

## Objects without a prototype

`Object.create(null)` makes an object with no prototype at all. It has no inherited methods, so names like `toString` or `constructor` don''t collide with anything. This is handy for dictionaries that hold arbitrary keys.

```js run
const dict = Object.create(null);
dict.apple = 3;

console.log("apple" in dict, "toString" in dict, Object.getPrototypeOf(dict));
```

The `in` operator checks the whole chain. On an ordinary object, `"toString" in {}` is `true`, because `toString` is inherited from `Object.prototype`. A null-prototype object returns `false`, and `Object.getPrototypeOf` returns `null`.

## Challenge: find the owner

Given an object and a key, find the object in its prototype chain that actually owns the key, or return `null` if no link has it. You''ll need `Object.hasOwn` and `Object.getPrototypeOf`.

```js exercise id=find-owner title="Find the owner"
function findOwner(obj, key) {
  // TODO: walk obj and its prototypes. Return the first object that owns `key`,
  // or null if none of them does.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function findOwner(obj, key) {
  let current = obj;
  while (current !== null) {
    if (Object.hasOwn(current, key)) {
      return current;
    }
    current = Object.getPrototypeOf(current);
  }
  return null;
}
--- test
const grandparent = { color: "red" };
const parent = Object.create(grandparent);
parent.size = 2;
const child = Object.create(parent);
child.name = "box";
assert(findOwner(child, "name") === child, "An own property should be found on the object itself");
assert(findOwner(child, "size") === parent, "size should be found on the parent");
assert(findOwner(child, "color") === grandparent, "color should be found two links up");
assert(findOwner(child, "missing") === null, "A key that nobody owns should give null");
assert(findOwner(Object.create(null), "x") === null, "A null-prototype object has no chain to search");
--- hint
Start with `obj`. If it owns `key`, you''re done. Otherwise move to its prototype. Stop when the prototype is `null`.
--- hint
Use a `while` loop over `current`. Check `Object.hasOwn(current, key)`, and set `current = Object.getPrototypeOf(current)` at the end of each step.
```

## Challenge: copy only the own entries

`for...in` is a common way to list an object''s keys, and it has a trap. It visits inherited enumerable properties as well as own ones. Write a function that returns `[key, value]` pairs for own properties only.

```js exercise id=own-entries title="Own entries only"
function ownEntries(obj) {
  // TODO: return an array of [key, value] pairs for the properties that obj owns.
  // Ignore anything inherited from its prototype.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function ownEntries(obj) {
  return Object.keys(obj).map((key) => [key, obj[key]]);
}
--- test
const base = { inherited: "yes" };
const item = Object.create(base);
item.a = 1;
item.b = "two";
assertEqual(ownEntries(item), [["a", 1], ["b", "two"]], "Only own properties should be listed");
assertEqual(ownEntries({}), [], "An empty object should give an empty list");
const fresh = { x: 9 };
assertEqual(ownEntries(fresh), [["x", 9]], "Plain objects should work the same way");
--- hint
`for...in` walks the whole chain. `Object.keys` lists only the object''s own enumerable keys.
--- hint
Call `Object.keys(obj)` and map each key to a pair `[key, obj[key]]`.
```

## Debug: one cart for every shopper

Each shopper should get an empty cart, but the carts share one list. The bug is about where the array lives. Fix it so every `Cart` owns its own `items`.

```js debug id=shared-cart-items title="Give each cart its own items"
function Cart() {}
Cart.prototype.items = [];
Cart.prototype.add = function (item) {
  this.items.push(item);
  return this.items.length;
};
--- solution
function Cart() {
  this.items = [];
}
Cart.prototype.add = function (item) {
  this.items.push(item);
  return this.items.length;
};
--- test
const first = new Cart();
const second = new Cart();
assertEqual(first.add("apple"), 1, "The first cart should hold one item");
assertEqual(second.add("pear"), 1, "A second cart must start empty, not share the first cart''s list");
assertEqual(first.items, ["apple"], "The first cart should keep only its own item");
assertEqual(second.items, ["pear"], "The second cart should keep only its own item");
--- hint
Run the code and add an item to two different carts. Look at where `items` is defined, and ask whether `this.items.push` creates a new property or reads one from the prototype.
--- hint
Initialize `items` in the constructor with `this.items = [];`. Each `new Cart()` then runs that line and gets its own array.
```

## Predict: shadowing versus shared objects

Two properties on the same chain behave differently. One is a number, which gets shadowed when you assign. The other is an object, which is shared when you change it through the child.

```js predict id=predict-shadow-vs-shared title="Predict the shared object"
const parent = { count: 1, nested: { hits: 0 } };
const child = Object.create(parent);
child.count += 10;
child.nested.hits += 5;
console.log([parent.count, child.count, parent.nested.hits].join(" "));
--- options
* 1 11 5
11 11 5
1 11 0
1 1 0
--- explain
`child.count += 10` reads the inherited `1`, adds 10, and assigns `11` as an own property of `child`, so the parent''s `count` stays `1`. `child.nested` is not assigned, so `hits += 5` reads the shared `nested` object through the chain and changes it. That object belongs to `parent`, so `parent.nested.hits` becomes `5`. The number was shadowed, and the object was shared.
```

## Recap

- Property lookup walks the prototype chain until it finds the property or reaches `null`.
- Assignment creates an own property on the object you assign to, which shadows an inherited value. It doesn''t modify the prototype.
- `Object.hasOwn` tests ownership, and `in` tests the whole chain. `for...in` also walks the chain, so use `Object.keys` when you want own properties only.
- Constructor functions put shared methods on `prototype`. Mutable per-instance state belongs in the constructor.
- Mutating a shared array or object through an inherited reference changes it for everyone who inherits from it.

**Up next:** classes in depth, which are a clearer syntax over this same prototype machinery, plus the private fields, getters and inheritance rules that the syntax adds.
', 0, '[]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_javascript_advanced_2_2', 'sec_javascript_advanced_2', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'Classes in depth: private fields, getters and inheritance', 'interactive', 1, 1440, NULL, NULL, 'The `class` keyword is often described as "syntax sugar for prototypes", and that''s accurate but incomplete. Classes change behavior in ways the old constructor functions could not: their bodies always run in strict mode, their methods are never enumerable, they can''t be called without `new`, and they gained real private state with `#` names. Those rules are the reason class-based code is easier to reason about, and they''re also the source of the errors you''ll hit when you forget one of them.

This lesson goes past the basics you already know. You''ll see how a class builds its prototype, how fields are initialized in a derived class (and why a base constructor can see the wrong values), how private state works and why it isn''t just a naming convention, and how to stop methods from losing their receiver. The examples focus on the rules that catch experienced developers.

## Classes are prototypes with better rules

A class declaration creates a constructor function whose `prototype` holds the methods. Everything from the last lesson still applies, but the class adds three guarantees. Methods are non-enumerable, so `for...in` and `Object.keys` skip them. The body runs in strict mode, so a lost `this` becomes `undefined` instead of the global object. And calling the class without `new` throws a `TypeError`.

```js run
class Account {
  #balance = 0;
  static count = 0;

  constructor(owner) {
    this.owner = owner;
    Account.count += 1;
  }

  deposit(amount) {
    if (amount <= 0) {
      throw new RangeError("Deposit must be positive");
    }
    this.#balance += amount;
    return this;
  }

  get balance() {
    return this.#balance;
  }
}

const acct = new Account("Ada");
acct.deposit(50).deposit(25);
console.log(acct.owner, acct.balance, Account.count);
console.log(Object.keys(acct));
```

`#balance` is a private field. Code outside the class can''t read or write it, and the `balance` getter is the only read path. `static count` belongs to the class itself, not to instances, so `Account.count` is the right place to read it. `deposit` returns `this`, which lets you chain calls. `Object.keys` shows only the public data, `owner`, and not `balance`.

> **Under the hood:** A private name like `#balance` is not a string key. Each class creates its own private names, so two classes that both use `#balance` never collide, and a subclass can''t reach its parent''s private fields by name. Private fields are stored per object in a hidden slot that only the defining class can access.

## Field initialization order

Fields are initialized in a precise sequence. In a base class, fields run before the constructor body. In a derived class, they run immediately after `super()` returns. That timing is the most common surprise with inheritance, and it shows up when a base constructor calls a method that a subclass overrides.

```js run
class Base {
  constructor() {
    this.log = this.describe();
  }
  describe() {
    return "base";
  }
}

class Derived extends Base {
  label = "derived-label";
  describe() {
    return "derived sees " + this.label;
  }
}

const d = new Derived();
console.log(d.log, d.label);
```

When `Base`''s constructor runs, `Derived`''s fields don''t exist yet. `this.describe()` still dispatches to `Derived.prototype.describe`, which reads `this.label` before the field is set, so it gets `undefined`. Only after `super()` returns is `label` assigned. The example prints the stale value and then the correct one.

> **Pitfall:** Don''t call overridable methods from a base constructor. The subclass hasn''t finished setting itself up, and the method sees a half-built object. Put that logic in an explicit `init()` call made after construction, or move the state into the constructor that owns it.

## Static members and brand checks

`static` members belong to the class. `static #name` is a private static field, and a static block runs once when the class is defined. Private names also support a check called a brand check: `#field in obj` is `true` only when `obj` was created by the class that declares that private field. It''s the reliable way to test "is this really a Temperature?" without relying on a property that anyone could fake.

```js run
class Temperature {
  static #instances = 0;
  #celsius;

  constructor(celsius) {
    this.#celsius = celsius;
    Temperature.#instances += 1;
  }

  static fromFahrenheit(f) {
    return new Temperature(((f - 32) * 5) / 9);
  }

  static get created() {
    return Temperature.#instances;
  }

  static isTemperature(value) {
    return #celsius in value;
  }

  get celsius() {
    return this.#celsius;
  }
}

const boiling = Temperature.fromFahrenheit(212);
console.log(boiling.celsius, Temperature.created, Temperature.isTemperature(boiling), Temperature.isTemperature({}));
```

`instanceof` checks the prototype chain, so it can be fooled by any object whose prototype was set to `Temperature.prototype`. The brand check can''t be fooled that way. An object only has `#celsius` if the constructor created it.

## Methods lose `this` just like functions

Class bodies are strict, which is good news for debugging: a detached method sees `undefined` rather than the global object. It still loses its receiver, though, and the fix is the same as with any function.

```js run
class Button {
  constructor(label) {
    this.label = label;
  }
  click() {
    return this.label + " clicked";
  }
}

const btn = new Button("OK");
const handler = btn.click;
try {
  handler();
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.name);
}
```

`handler()` runs `click` with `this` set to `undefined`, so `this.label` throws. Event handlers have this problem constantly. There are two standard fixes: bind the method in the constructor, or declare it as an arrow function class field, which captures the instance as `this`. You''ll practice the second one in the debug challenge below.

## Challenge: a stack with private storage

A stack keeps its items private, so callers can only use the operations you define. Build a `Stack` whose `items` array can''t be reached from outside.

```js exercise id=private-stack title="A stack with private storage"
class Stack {
  // TODO: store items in a private field, and implement push, pop, peek and a size getter.
  // push(x) returns the new size. pop() removes and returns the top item, or undefined.
  // peek() returns the top item without removing it, or undefined.
  push(item) {
    return null;
  }
}
--- solution
class Stack {
  #items = [];

  push(item) {
    this.#items.push(item);
    return this.#items.length;
  }

  pop() {
    return this.#items.pop();
  }

  peek() {
    return this.#items[this.#items.length - 1];
  }

  get size() {
    return this.#items.length;
  }
}
--- test
const s = new Stack();
assertEqual(s.push("a"), 1, "push should return the new size");
assertEqual(s.push("b"), 2, "push should return the new size again");
assertEqual(s.peek(), "b", "peek should return the top item");
assertEqual(s.size, 2, "size should count the items");
assertEqual(s.pop(), "b", "pop should return the top item");
assertEqual(s.pop(), "a", "pop should return the next item");
assertEqual(s.pop(), undefined, "pop on an empty stack should return undefined");
assertEqual(Object.keys(s), [], "Items must not be visible as own properties");
assertEqual(JSON.stringify(s), "{}", "Serializing the stack should reveal nothing private");
--- hint
Use a private field such as `#items = [];`. You can call `push`, `pop` and `length` on it from inside the class, and nowhere else.
--- hint
`push` and `pop` on the array do most of the work. `peek` reads the last element by index, and `size` is a getter that returns the array''s length.
```

## Challenge: extend and call the parent

Subclasses reuse behavior with `extends` and call the parent''s version with `super`. Build an `Animal` with a `speak` method, and a `Dog` that overrides it by first calling the parent''s version.

```js exercise id=dog-speaks title="Override and call super"
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  speak() {
    return this.name + " makes a sound";
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  // TODO: override speak so it returns the parent''s sentence followed by " and barks"
  speak() {
    return null;
  }
}
--- solution
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  speak() {
    return this.name + " makes a sound";
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  speak() {
    return super.speak() + " and barks";
  }
}
--- test
const rex = new Dog("Rex");
assertEqual(rex.speak(), "Rex makes a sound and barks", "Dog.speak should extend the parent''s sentence");
assert(rex instanceof Dog, "rex should be a Dog");
assert(rex instanceof Animal, "rex should also be an Animal");
assertEqual(new Dog("Fido").name, "Fido", "The name should come from the parent constructor");
--- hint
`super.speak()` calls the parent''s version of the method, with the current object as `this`.
--- hint
Return `super.speak()` concatenated with `" and barks"`. You don''t need to call the parent constructor, since `Dog` inherits it automatically.
```

## Debug: a timer that loses itself

This class should count down when you call `tick` as a standalone function, for example when it''s passed to `setTimeout` or to an event system. It throws instead. Fix the method so the receiver can''t be lost.

```js debug id=timer-tick-arrow title="Keep tick bound"
class Countdown {
  constructor(seconds) {
    this.seconds = seconds;
  }
  tick() {
    this.seconds -= 1;
    return this.seconds;
  }
}
--- solution
class Countdown {
  constructor(seconds) {
    this.seconds = seconds;
  }
  tick = () => {
    this.seconds -= 1;
    return this.seconds;
  };
}
--- test
const timer = new Countdown(3);
const tick = timer.tick;
assertEqual(tick(), 2, "A detached tick should count down from 3 to 2");
assertEqual(tick(), 1, "A second detached tick should count to 1");
assertEqual(timer.seconds, 1, "The original instance should carry the updated value");
assertEqual(new Countdown(5).tick(), 4, "Normal method calls should still work");
--- hint
Run it and read the error. Then check what `this` is when `tick` is called without a receiver, which is how `const tick = timer.tick` calls it.
--- hint
A class field can hold an arrow function, which captures `this` from the instance it''s created in. Turn `tick()` into a field whose value is an arrow function.
```

## Predict: the order of constructors

Watch the order of events, because it is the most useful model for derived classes. The derived constructor runs before `super()`, the base constructor runs next, and the subclass''s field initializers run after `super()` returns.

```js predict id=predict-field-order title="Predict the constructor order"
const log = [];
class A {
  constructor() {
    log.push("A constructor");
    this.init();
  }
  init() {
    log.push("A init");
  }
}
class B extends A {
  field = log.push("B field");
  constructor() {
    log.push("B before super");
    super();
    log.push("B after super");
  }
  init() {
    log.push("B init");
  }
}
new B();
console.log(log.join(", "));
--- options
* B before super, A constructor, B init, B field, B after super
B before super, A constructor, B field, B init, B after super
A constructor, B init, B before super, B field, B after super
B before super, B field, A constructor, B init, B after super
--- explain
`B`''s constructor starts first, so `"B before super"` is logged. `super()` runs `A`''s constructor, which calls `this.init()`. Because `init` is overridden, that call reaches `B.prototype.init`, which logs `"B init"`, before `B`''s fields exist. Only after `super()` returns does `B` initialize its field, logging `"B field"`, and then `"B after super"` runs. `"A init"` never appears, because the override replaced it.
```

## Recap

- A class builds its prototype with non-enumerable methods, runs in strict mode, and can''t be called without `new`.
- `#private` fields and methods are real privacy, enforced by the engine. `#field in obj` checks that an object was created by the class that declares that field.
- Derived fields are initialized after `super()` returns. A base constructor that calls an overridden method sees the subclass before it''s ready.
- A method loses its receiver when it''s detached. Bind it in the constructor or make it an arrow-function field.
- Use `super.method()` to extend a parent''s behavior instead of copying it.

**Up next:** iterators and generators, which let a function pause in the middle of its work and hand back one value at a time.
