('les_javascript_advanced_2_5', 'sec_javascript_advanced_2', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'Checkpoint: objects under the hood', 'quiz', 4, 420, NULL, NULL, 'Answer all questions — you need **70%** to pass. You can retake the quiz as many times as you like.', 0, '[{"id":"q1","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nconst base = { greet() { return \"hi\"; } };\nconst obj = Object.create(base);\nobj.name = \"x\";\nconsole.log(Object.keys(obj).length, obj.greet === base.greet);\n```","options":["0 true","1 true","2 false","1 false"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`obj` has one own property, `name`, so `Object.keys` returns one key. `greet` isn''t copied onto `obj`. Lookup finds it on `base`, so `obj.greet` is the same function as `base.greet`. The tempting `1 false` assumes inherited methods are copied rather than shared."},{"id":"q2","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nconst parent = { list: [], n: 0 };\nconst child = Object.create(parent);\nchild.n += 1;\nchild.list.push(\"a\");\nconsole.log(parent.n, parent.list.length);\n```","options":["1 1","0 0","0 1","1 0"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`child.n += 1` reads the inherited `0`, then assigns `1` as an own property of `child`, which shadows the parent''s value and leaves `parent.n` at `0`. `child.list.push` never assigns to `list`, so it mutates the shared array that `parent` owns. The lesson is that numbers get shadowed on write, but objects are shared through the chain."},{"id":"q3","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nclass Base {\n  constructor() {\n    this.x = this.compute();\n  }\n  compute() {\n    return 1;\n  }\n}\nclass Child extends Base {\n  base = 10;\n  compute() {\n    return this.base;\n  }\n}\nconsole.log(new Child().x);\n```","options":["10","1","undefined","NaN"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`Child`''s fields are initialized after `super()` returns. While `Base`''s constructor is running, `Child.prototype.compute` is already the method being called, but `this.base` doesn''t exist yet, so it reads `undefined`. The value `1` would come from `Base.prototype.compute`, which `Child` overrides, so it never runs."},{"id":"q4","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nclass Vault {\n  #secret = 42;\n}\nconsole.log(Object.keys(new Vault()).length, \"secret\" in new Vault());\n```","options":["1 true","0 true","1 false","0 false"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"The field is private, so it doesn''t appear in `Object.keys`. The `in` operator checks for a string property named `secret`, and the private field is named `#secret` in a separate namespace, so `\"secret\" in` is `false`. Private fields are invisible to reflection and to any string-keyed check."},{"id":"q5","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nconst p = new Proxy(\n  { a: 1 },\n  { get: (t, k) => (k in t ? t[k] : \"missing\") }\n);\nconsole.log(p.a, p.b);\n```","options":["1 undefined","1 missing","undefined missing","TypeError"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The `get` trap runs for every read through the proxy. For `a`, the key exists, so the handler returns `1`. For `b`, the trap returns the fallback string `\"missing\"`. The option `1 undefined` is what you''d get without the proxy, which shows the trap really did run."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nconst frozen = Object.freeze({ n: 1 });\nconsole.log(Reflect.set(frozen, \"n\", 2), frozen.n);\n```","options":["true 2","true 1","false 1","false 2"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`Reflect.set` returns a boolean instead of throwing. A frozen object refuses the write, so `Reflect.set` returns `false`, and `frozen.n` still holds `1`. Plain assignment would also fail, but in sloppy mode it does so silently, which is why `Reflect` is the clearer way to test a write."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nfunction* gen() {\n  console.log(\"started\");\n  yield 1;\n}\nconst it = gen();\nconsole.log(\"created\");\nit.next();\n```","options":["created, then started","started, then created","only created","only started"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Calling a generator function returns a generator object and doesn''t run the body. `created` prints first. The body runs only when `it.next()` is called, and then `started` prints before the `yield` pauses it. Calling `gen()` is lazy, which is what makes generators good for infinite or expensive sequences."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_javascript_advanced_3_1', 'sec_javascript_advanced_3', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'Promises: states, chaining and errors', 'interactive', 0, 1560, NULL, NULL, 'Almost every interesting piece of JavaScript code waits for something: a network response, a file, a timer, a user''s click. A promise is the object that stands for a result that doesn''t exist yet. It''s the handle you pass around, attach callbacks to, and combine with other handles, so the code can say "when this value arrives, do that" instead of blocking or nesting callbacks ten levels deep.

Promises are also where most asynchronous bugs begin. A chain that forgets to `return` a value silently passes `undefined` down the line. A `catch` that doesn''t rethrow hides a failure from everyone upstream. A `new Promise` wrapped around another promise adds a layer that is never needed. This lesson gives you the exact states, the rules for chaining and error propagation, and the traps that come up in real code.

## Three states, one decision

A promise is always in one of three states. It starts **pending**. It becomes **fulfilled** with a value, or **rejected** with a reason. Once it leaves pending, it''s **settled**, and it never changes again. That one-way rule is what makes promises safe to share: every consumer sees the same result, however late it attaches.

The executor you pass to `new Promise` runs immediately and synchronously. It calls `resolve(value)` or `reject(reason)` to settle the promise.

```js run
const ready = new Promise((resolve) => {
  console.log("executor runs now");
  resolve("ok");
  console.log("still inside the executor");
});

ready.then((value) => console.log("value:", value));
console.log("after then");
```

The executor''s two logs run before `then` is even called, so the first two lines are synchronous. The callback passed to `then` runs later, after the current script has finished, which is why `"after then"` comes before `"value:"`. A promise''s callbacks are always asynchronous, even when the promise is already settled. You''ll see exactly why in lesson 11.

> **Pitfall:** Calling `resolve` or `reject` doesn''t stop the executor. Any code after the call still runs, and a `throw` after `resolve` is silently ignored, because the promise is already settled. Return right after you settle, when the code after that point should not run.

## Chaining: each `then` returns a new promise

`then`, `catch` and `finally` each return a new promise. Whatever your callback returns becomes the value of that new promise. If you return another promise, the chain waits for it and then adopts its result, which is called flattening. That''s how a sequence of asynchronous steps becomes a flat chain instead of a pyramid.

```js run
const doubledPlusOne = Promise.resolve(10)
  .then((n) => n * 2)
  .then((n) => n + 1);

doubledPlusOne.then((value) => console.log("chain result:", value));
console.log("chain started");
```

Each `then` receives the value from the step before it. The final value is available only at the end of the chain. The script prints `"chain started"` first, because every step runs asynchronously.

> **Pitfall:** A callback with braces and no `return` returns `undefined`, and the next step receives `undefined`. This is the single most common promise bug. An arrow function with an expression body returns automatically, so the braces are where the bug hides.

## Errors travel down the chain

A rejection skips every `then` callback until it reaches a handler for rejections, either a `catch` or the second argument of `then`. A handler that returns normally turns the chain back into fulfilled, so later steps run again.

```js run
const steps = [];
Promise.reject(new Error("disk full"))
  .then(() => steps.push("skipped"))
  .catch((err) => steps.push("caught: " + err.message))
  .then(() => console.log(steps.join(", ")));
```

The `then` in the middle is skipped entirely, because the chain is rejected. The `catch` handles the error, pushes a message, and returns `undefined`, which fulfills the chain again. So the final `then` runs normally. That''s the intended design: a `catch` recovers. If you want to report and continue failing, throw again inside the handler.

> **Pitfall:** `then(onSuccess, onFailure)` and `then(onSuccess).catch(onFailure)` are not the same. In the first form, `onFailure` handles only errors from the earlier promise, not errors thrown by `onSuccess`. In the second, the `catch` also handles errors from `onSuccess`.

## Two ticks for a returned promise

Returning a promise from `then` costs more than returning a plain value, because the chain has to wait for the inner promise and then copy its result. You can see the difference in ordering with two chains that start at the same time.

```js run
Promise.resolve(1)
  .then((n) => Promise.resolve(n + 1))
  .then((n) => console.log("flattened:", n));

Promise.reject(new Error("x")).then(null, (err) => console.log("handled by second arg:", err.message));
```

Both chains start immediately. The second one handles its rejection in its first callback, while the first chain needs more steps to adopt the inner promise. So the second message prints first, even though it was written second. The exact order is decided by the rules of the microtask queue, which lesson 11 explains.

## `finally` passes the value through

`finally` runs whether the promise fulfilled or rejected, and it takes no value. Its callback can''t change the result. Whatever came in goes out, so `finally` is for cleanup like hiding a spinner, not for transforming data.

```js run
Promise.resolve("data")
  .finally(() => console.log("finally runs, value is ignored"))
  .then((value) => console.log("still have:", value));
```

The `finally` callback runs first and its return value is discarded. The `then` after it still receives `"data"`, because `finally` returns the original value.

## Challenge: a delay you can await

Write a function that returns a promise which resolves to a value after a short delay. It''s the building block for almost every timing demo in the rest of the course.

```js exercise id=delay-value title="A delay that resolves"
function delay(ms, value) {
  // TODO: return a promise that resolves to `value` after `ms` milliseconds.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function delay(ms, value) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms);
  });
}
--- test
const pending = delay(10, "hi");
assert(pending instanceof Promise, "delay should return a Promise");
assertEqual(await pending, "hi", "The promise should resolve to the given value");
assertEqual(await delay(5), undefined, "With no value, it should resolve to undefined");
--- hint
Create a `new Promise`. Inside its executor, call `setTimeout` with a callback that calls `resolve` with the value.
--- hint
`setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms)` runs the resolve after the delay. Return the promise from the function, and don''t call `resolve` synchronously.
```

## Challenge: wrap an error with context

A function that loads a profile receives a fetcher, which returns a promise of a user. Return the upper-cased name on success. On failure, reject with a new `Error` whose message says what failed and keeps the original message. Use a promise chain, not `async`/`await`, since that''s the point of this lesson.

```js exercise id=profile-errors title="Add context to a failure"
function loadProfile(fetcher) {
  // TODO: call fetcher(). On success, resolve to user.name in upper case.
  // On failure, reject with new Error("profile failed: " + original message).
  return null;
}
--- solution
function loadProfile(fetcher) {
  return fetcher()
    .then((user) => user.name.toUpperCase())
    .catch((err) => {
      throw new Error("profile failed: " + err.message);
    });
}
--- test
assertEqual(await loadProfile(() => Promise.resolve({ name: "ada" })), "ADA", "A successful load should return the upper-cased name");
let message = null;
try {
  await loadProfile(() => Promise.reject(new Error("boom")));
} catch (err) {
  message = err.message;
}
assertEqual(message, "profile failed: boom", "A failed fetch should reject with added context");
--- hint
`fetcher()` returns a promise. You can chain `.then` for the success case and `.catch` for the failure case.
--- hint
In the `catch`, throw a new `Error` that includes `err.message`. Throwing inside a `catch` rejects the chain, so the caller sees the new error.
```

## Debug: the chain that forgot to return

This chain should report the square of a number. It reports `undefined` instead. The bug is one missing word, and the symptom only appears in the final result, several steps away from the cause.

```js debug id=missing-return title="Fix the missing return"
function squareThenLabel(n) {
  return Promise.resolve(n)
    .then((value) => {
      value * value;
    })
    .then((squared) => "square is " + squared);
}
--- solution
function squareThenLabel(n) {
  return Promise.resolve(n)
    .then((value) => {
      return value * value;
    })
    .then((squared) => "square is " + squared);
}
--- test
assertEqual(await squareThenLabel(3), "square is 9", "squareThenLabel(3) should be ''square is 9''");
assertEqual(await squareThenLabel(7), "square is 49", "The square should follow the input, not a fixed value");
--- hint
Run it and read the final value. Then trace where the number goes: does the first `then` pass anything to the second one?
--- hint
A block-bodied arrow function only returns what you write with `return`. Return `value * value` from the first callback.
```

## Predict: callbacks in order

Synchronous code, `then` callbacks and the executor all interleave in a specific way. Trace the order carefully, and remember that each `then` callback is queued when its promise is already settled.

```js predict id=predict-promise-order title="Predict the promise order"
const log = [];
Promise.resolve("a").then((v) => log.push(v));
log.push("b");
new Promise((resolve) => {
  log.push("c");
  resolve("d");
}).then((v) => log.push(v));
log.push("e");
setTimeout(() => console.log(log.join(" ")), 0);
--- options
* b c e a d
a b c e d
b c e d a
c b e a d
--- explain
Everything before the first `then` callback runs synchronously: `"b"` is pushed, then the executor pushes `"c"`, and `"e"` is pushed. The two `then` callbacks run afterward, as microtasks, in the order they were queued. `Promise.resolve("a")` was already settled when its `then` was called, so `"a"` is queued first. The second promise is settled inside its executor, so `"d"` is queued second. The `setTimeout` callback runs last of all, so it prints the complete log.
```

## Recap

- A promise is pending until it settles, once, as fulfilled with a value or rejected with a reason. The executor runs synchronously, and `then` callbacks always run asynchronously.
- `then`, `catch` and `finally` return new promises. A returned value becomes the next value, and a returned promise is flattened into it.
- Rejections skip `then` callbacks until a `catch` or a rejection handler recovers. A recovered chain continues normally.
- A block-bodied callback returns `undefined` unless you write `return`. That silent `undefined` is the most common promise bug.
- `finally` runs cleanup and passes the original value or error through unchanged.

**Up next:** `async` and `await`, which let you write promise chains as if they were ordinary sequential code, along with the error handling and the missing-`await` traps that come with them.
', 0, '[]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_javascript_advanced_3_2', 'sec_javascript_advanced_3', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'async/await: writing async code that reads like sync code', 'interactive', 1, 1440, NULL, NULL, 'Promise chains are correct, but they make you think in callbacks. Every step lives in its own function, loops become recursion or `reduce`, and an error has to be threaded through `catch` at the right point. `async` and `await` keep the same promises underneath and let you write the code in order. A line that says `const user = await loadUser(id)` reads like sequential code, pauses the function until the value arrives, and then continues with a plain variable.

The convenience hides a few details you need to know. An `async` function always returns a promise, even when it returns a plain value. `await` pauses only the function it''s in, not the whole program. A forgotten `await` silently gives you a promise where you expected a value. And an `await` inside a loop makes the work sequential, which may be far slower than you intended. This lesson covers those rules, along with the error handling patterns that actually work.

## An async function returns a promise

Marking a function `async` does two things. It makes `await` legal inside the body, and it wraps the function''s return value in a promise. A value returned from an async function is delivered to the caller through that promise, and a thrown error becomes a rejection.

```js run
async function fetchScore(name) {
  return name.length * 10;
}

async function main() {
  const score = await fetchScore("Ada");
  console.log("score:", score);
}

main();
console.log("main started");
```

`main()` runs synchronously until its first `await`, and then it returns a promise. That''s why `"main started"` prints before `"score:"`. The rest of `main` resumes later, once the awaited promise has settled. Calling an async function never blocks the caller.

> **Under the hood:** `await` splits the function in two. The code before the `await` runs now. The code after it becomes a continuation, which is queued as a microtask when the awaited promise settles. The engine keeps the function''s local variables alive between the two halves, which is why `score` survives the pause.

## Sequential and concurrent are different programs

`await` waits for one thing at a time. Two awaits in a row run one after the other, which is what you want when the second request depends on the first. It''s a mistake when the two requests are independent, because you pay for both delays in series.

```js run
const later = (ms, label) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(label), ms));

async function sequentialTotal() {
  const start = Date.now();
  await later(30, "A");
  await later(30, "B");
  return Date.now() - start >= 50 ? "took at least 50 ms" : "too fast";
}

sequentialTotal().then(console.log);
```

Two 30 ms waits in a row can''t finish in less than 60 ms, so the check passes. The code doesn''t print a measured time, which would differ on every run. If the two waits were independent, you could start both before awaiting either, and you''d pay for only the slower one. You''ll learn to do that properly with `Promise.all` in lesson 12.

> **Pitfall:** An `await` inside a `for` loop serializes the loop. Each iteration waits for the previous one, even when the iterations have nothing to do with each other. Sequential is right for dependent steps, and wrong for a batch of independent requests.

> **In the wild:** A dashboard that loads users, orders and metrics should start all three requests before it awaits any of them. Three sequential awaits turn a 300 ms page load into nearly a second, and nobody notices until the page is in production.

## Error handling uses try and catch

A rejected promise throws at the `await` that waits for it, so `try`/`catch` works exactly as it does for synchronous code. That''s the main advantage of `await` over chained `.catch`: the error handling sits next to the code that can fail.

```js run
async function risky(flag) {
  if (flag) {
    throw new Error("network down");
  }
  return "fine";
}

async function attempt(flag) {
  try {
    return await risky(flag);
  } catch (err) {
    return "recovered from " + err.message;
  }
}

attempt(true).then(console.log);
attempt(false).then(console.log);
```

Both calls succeed as functions. The first `catch` handles the rejection, and the second returns the value normally.

> **Pitfall:** `return risky(flag)` without `await` inside a `try` doesn''t catch anything. The function returns the promise, the `try` block exits immediately, and the rejection goes to whoever calls `attempt`. Here''s the proof:

```js run
async function leaky() {
  try {
    return risky(true);
  } catch (err) {
    return "caught";
  }
}

leaky().catch((err) => console.log("escaped:", err.message));
```

The `try` finished before the rejection happened, so `catch` never ran. Always write `return await` when you want the local `catch` to see the error.

## A missing `await` returns a promise

Forgetting `await` is the most common async bug, and it doesn''t throw. The expression evaluates to a promise, and a promise is always truthy, so a check on it always passes.

```js run
async function isOpen() {
  return false;
}

async function enter() {
  if (isOpen()) {
    return "walk in";
  }
  return "closed";
}

enter().then(console.log);
```

`isOpen()` returns a promise, which is truthy, so `enter` says `"walk in"` even though the door is closed. The fix is `await isOpen()`. You''ll practice exactly that bug in the debug challenge below.

## `forEach` doesn''t wait for async callbacks

`forEach` calls its callback and ignores what it returns. An `async` callback returns a promise, which `forEach` discards, so nothing waits for the work inside it.

```js run
async function saveAll(items) {
  const saved = [];
  items.forEach(async (item) => {
    await null;
    saved.push(item);
  });
  return saved.length;
}

saveAll(["a", "b"]).then((n) => console.log("saved count right away:", n));
```

The function returns before any of the callbacks has resumed, so the count is `0`. Use `for...of` with `await` for sequential work, or `Promise.all` with `map` for concurrent work. Both let the function wait for every item.

## Challenge: retry until it works

Write an async function that calls `fn` up to `attempts` times. It returns the first successful result. If every attempt fails, it throws the error from the last one. Remember that a rejected `await` must be caught inside the loop to try again.

```js exercise id=retry-until-success title="Retry an async call"
async function retry(fn, attempts) {
  // TODO: call fn() up to `attempts` times. Return the first result that succeeds.
  // If all attempts fail, throw the error from the last attempt.
  return null;
}
--- solution
async function retry(fn, attempts) {
  let lastError;
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      lastError = err;
    }
  }
  throw lastError;
}
--- test
let calls = 0;
const flaky = async () => {
  calls += 1;
  if (calls < 3) {
    throw new Error("try " + calls);
  }
  return "ok on " + calls;
};
assertEqual(await retry(flaky, 5), "ok on 3", "retry should return the first successful result");
assertEqual(calls, 3, "retry should stop calling fn once it succeeds");

let count = 0;
const broken = async () => {
  count += 1;
  throw new Error("fail " + count);
};
let message = null;
try {
  await retry(broken, 2);
} catch (err) {
  message = err.message;
}
assertEqual(message, "fail 2", "When every attempt fails, the last error should be thrown");
assertEqual(count, 2, "retry should make exactly the allowed number of attempts");
--- hint
Use a `for` loop that runs `attempts` times. Inside it, `try` to `await fn()` and return the result. Keep the caught error in a variable outside the loop.
--- hint
`return await fn();` inside `try` makes the rejection land in your `catch`. After the loop, `throw lastError;` gives the caller the final failure.
```

## Challenge: run tasks one after another

Write an async function that runs an array of task functions in order, waits for each to finish before starting the next, and returns their results in the same order. Each task returns a promise.

```js exercise id=run-in-order title="Run tasks in order"
async function runInOrder(tasks) {
  // TODO: run each task in turn, waiting for it to finish before starting the next.
  // Return an array of the results, in the same order as the tasks.
  return null;
}
--- solution
async function runInOrder(tasks) {
  const results = [];
  for (const task of tasks) {
    results.push(await task());
  }
  return results;
}
--- test
const seen = [];
const slow = () =>
  new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      seen.push("slow");
      resolve("one");
    }, 30);
  });
const fast = () =>
  new Promise((resolve) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      seen.push("fast");
      resolve("two");
    }, 5);
  });
assertEqual(await runInOrder([slow, fast]), ["one", "two"], "Results should come back in task order");
assertEqual(seen, ["slow", "fast"], "Tasks must finish one at a time, so the slow one finishes first");
assertEqual(await runInOrder([]), [], "An empty list should give an empty array");
--- hint
Loop over `tasks` with `for...of`. Inside the loop, `await task()` and push its result into an array.
--- hint
Each iteration should wait for the previous task to finish. Don''t call `tasks.map(...)` here, because `map` starts every task right away.
```

## Debug: the check that always passes

This function should deny deletion unless the user is an admin. It allows every user, because `isAllowed` returns a promise and the `if` treats any promise as true. Fix it so the check waits for the answer.

```js debug id=missing-await-check title="Await the permission check"
async function isAllowed(user) {
  return user.role === "admin";
}
async function deleteRecord(user, id) {
  if (isAllowed(user)) {
    return "deleted " + id;
  }
  return "denied";
}
--- solution
async function isAllowed(user) {
  return user.role === "admin";
}
async function deleteRecord(user, id) {
  if (await isAllowed(user)) {
    return "deleted " + id;
  }
  return "denied";
}
--- test
assertEqual(await deleteRecord({ role: "admin" }, 7), "deleted 7", "Admins should be allowed to delete");
assertEqual(await deleteRecord({ role: "guest" }, 7), "denied", "Non-admins must be denied");
--- hint
Run it with a guest user and look at the result. Then ask what `isAllowed(user)` returns, and whether an `if` can tell a promise from a value.
--- hint
Put `await` in front of the call to `isAllowed` inside the `if`. Then the condition sees the boolean.
```

## Predict: where the continuation runs

Each `step` runs until its `await`, then yields. Work out which lines run synchronously and which resume afterward.

```js predict id=predict-await-order title="Predict the await order"
const log = [];
async function step(name) {
  log.push(name + " start");
  await null;
  log.push(name + " resumed");
}
step("A");
step("B");
log.push("main");
setTimeout(() => console.log(log.join(", ")), 0);
--- options
* A start, B start, main, A resumed, B resumed
A start, A resumed, B start, B resumed, main
main, A start, B start, A resumed, B resumed
A start, B start, A resumed, B resumed, main
--- explain
Calling `step("A")` runs its body until `await null`, logging `"A start"`, and then returns a promise. The same happens for `"B"`. Back in the main script, `"main"` is logged. Only then do the continuations run as microtasks, in the order the awaits were reached, so `"A resumed"` and then `"B resumed"`. The `setTimeout` callback comes last, so the whole log is printed in one line.
```

## Recap

- An `async` function always returns a promise. Its return value resolves that promise, and a thrown error rejects it.
- `await` pauses the enclosing function until the promise settles. The code before it runs synchronously when the function is called.
- Sequential `await`s run one after another. Use `Promise.all` (lesson 12) when the work is independent.
- Put `await` before `return` inside a `try` block to catch the rejection locally.
- A missing `await` leaves you holding a promise, which is always truthy. `forEach` never waits for an async callback, so use `for...of` for sequential work.

**Up next:** the event loop, which decides the exact order in which microtasks, timers and rendering run, and explains everything you''ve seen in the predictions so far.
