('les_javascript_advanced_6_3', 'sec_javascript_advanced_6', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'Checkpoint: functional style & debugging', 'quiz', 2, 420, NULL, NULL, 'Answer all questions — you need **70%** to pass. You can retake the quiz as many times as you like.', 0, '[{"id":"q1","prompt":"What happens when you run `[].reduce((a, b) => a + b)`?","options":["It returns 0","It returns undefined","It throws a TypeError","It returns NaN"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Without a seed, reduce uses the first element as the starting accumulator. An empty array has no first element, so the call throws a TypeError. Passing 0 as the seed makes the empty case return 0 instead."},{"id":"q2","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nconsole.log([''1'', ''2'', ''3''].map(parseInt));\n```","options":["[ 1, 2, 3 ]","[ 1, NaN, NaN ]","[ NaN, NaN, NaN ]","[ 1, 2, NaN ]"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"map calls its callback with (item, index, array), so parseInt receives the index as its radix. Radix 1 is invalid and radix 2 cannot read the digit 3, so the second and third results are NaN. Wrap the call as (s) => parseInt(s, 10) to pass only the intended argument."},{"id":"q3","prompt":"What is the result of `pipe(x => x + 1, x => x * 10)(1)` when pipe runs its steps from left to right?","options":["20","11","2","10"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"pipe applies the first step to the input, giving 2, then the second step, giving 20. A compose that ran right to left would give 11 instead. Naming the direction you write matters, because the same two functions produce different answers in each order."},{"id":"q4","prompt":"A curried helper uses `fn.length` to decide when enough arguments have arrived. What are the lengths of `(a, b, c) => a + b + c` and of that function bound with `bind(null, 1)`?","options":["3 and 3","2 and 2","3 and 2","2 and 3"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A function''s length counts its declared parameters, so the arrow has length 3. bind fixes leading arguments and reports the remaining count, so binding one argument gives length 2. Default and rest parameters change length as well, which is why a curry built on length needs care."},{"id":"q5","prompt":"What does this function return when called?\n\n```js\nfunction f() {\n  try {\n    return 1;\n  } finally {\n    return 2;\n  }\n}\n```","options":["1","undefined","It throws an error","2"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"The try block computes 1, but the finally block runs before the function exits and its return replaces the pending value. A return inside finally also cancels any exception that was in flight, so it should be reserved for deliberate overrides."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"What does this print?\n\n```js\nconst fns = [];\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) fns.push(() => i);\nconsole.log(fns.map((f) => f()));\n```","options":["[ 0, 1, 2 ]","[ 1, 2, 3 ]","[ 3, 3, 3 ]","[ undefined, undefined, undefined ]"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"var creates one variable for the whole loop, and every closure reads it after the loop has finished, when i is 3. Replacing var with let gives each iteration its own binding, so the closures would return 0, 1 and 2."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"What is `new Error(''x'', { cause: ''y'' }).cause`?","options":["undefined","x","y","The Error object itself"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The second argument to the Error constructor takes an options object, and its cause property is stored on the error. The message ''x'' is separate. Attaching the original error as cause is how a wrapper keeps the underlying failure visible for debugging."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_javascript_advanced_7_1', 'sec_javascript_advanced_7', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'Project: an async task queue with a concurrency limit', 'interactive', 0, 2400, NULL, NULL, 'Real applications issue far more asynchronous work than they can afford to run at once. An image pipeline with a thousand thumbnails, a crawler, a batch import that calls a rate-limited API: each one needs a queue that starts work in order, runs a bounded number of tasks in parallel, keeps going after failures and reports results to whoever asked. Promises give you the pieces, but the coordination (who starts next, what happens when one fails, when the queue is really empty) is your code to write.

In this capstone you''ll build a `TaskQueue` in four stages. Each stage adds one idea: running a task safely, limiting concurrency, collecting results when some tasks fail, and then assembling everything into one class with pause and idle support. Every stage has its own challenge, and the last one is an integrated test of the whole class. Read each stage''s run cells before you start its challenge, because they show the behavior the tests check.

## Why not just `Promise.all`?

The first instinct is to start everything and wait for all of it. That works until the list is long. The cell below starts four jobs at once, and the peak number of in-flight jobs is the number of jobs:

```js run
let inFlight = 0;
let peak = 0;
const job = async (n) => {
  inFlight++;
  peak = Math.max(peak, inFlight);
  await null;
  inFlight--;
  return n;
};

const all = await Promise.all([1, 2, 3, 4].map(job));
console.log(all, "peak:", peak);
```

`Promise.all` has no limit. With a thousand tasks, you''d open a thousand connections or allocate a thousand buffers simultaneously. The queue''s job is to cap that number, and to keep the rest waiting in order without anyone polling for free slots.

Two more facts shape the design. Promises settle asynchronously, so a task should never start in the middle of the `add` call that scheduled it. And a rejected task is a result, not a crash: the queue has to report it to its caller and keep going.

```js run
const order = [];
Promise.resolve().then(() => order.push("microtask"));
order.push("sync");
console.log(order);
```

The log shows only `sync`. The microtask runs after the current code finishes, which is why the queue can record a task as running before the task''s own code begins.

## Stage 1: run a task safely

A task is a function that returns a value or a promise. Your first helper has two jobs. It must start the task asynchronously, and it must convert both a synchronous `throw` and a rejected promise into a rejection that the caller can handle. A `Promise.resolve().then(...)` gives you both, because an exception inside `then` becomes a rejection.

```js exercise id=run-task title="Run a task asynchronously and safely"
function runTask(task) {
  // TODO: return a promise for the task''s result.
  // The task must not start synchronously. A synchronous throw or a rejected promise
  // from the task should reject the returned promise. An async task should be awaited.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function runTask(task) {
  return Promise.resolve().then(() => task());
}
--- test
let started = false;
const pending = runTask(() => {
  started = true;
  return 6 * 7;
});
assert(started === false, "The task should not start synchronously");
assertEqual(await pending, 42, "The promise should resolve with the task''s result");
assertEqual(await runTask(async () => "async ok"), "async ok", "Async tasks should be awaited");
let message = null;
try {
  await runTask(() => {
    throw new Error("nope");
  });
} catch (err) {
  message = err.message;
}
assertEqual(message, "nope", "A synchronous throw should reject the promise");
--- hint
Wrap the call in a promise chain. `Promise.resolve().then(callback)` runs `callback` asynchronously, and anything it throws or returns (including a promise) flows into the result.
--- hint
`return Promise.resolve().then(() => task());` is the whole function. The arrow matters: it calls `task` inside the chain, so the call itself is deferred.
```

## Stage 2: limit concurrency with a FIFO waiting list

A bounded queue keeps two things: how many tasks are running, and a list of tasks waiting for a slot. `add` puts a task on the waiting list and tries to start it. When a task finishes, it frees its slot and starts the next waiting task. Tasks start in the order they were added, and a rejected task frees its slot the same way a successful one does.

```js exercise id=create-queue title="A queue with a concurrency limit"
function createQueue(concurrency) {
  // TODO: return an object with add(task). add() returns a promise for that task''s result.
  // At most `concurrency` tasks run at once, and waiting tasks start in the order they were added.
  // A task that throws must reject its own promise and must not block the queue.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function createQueue(concurrency) {
  const waiting = [];
  let active = 0;
  const next = () => {
    if (active >= concurrency || waiting.length === 0) return;
    const { task, resolve, reject } = waiting.shift();
    active++;
    Promise.resolve()
      .then(task)
      .then(resolve, reject)
      .finally(() => {
        active--;
        next();
      });
    next();
  };
  return {
    add(task) {
      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        waiting.push({ task, resolve, reject });
        next();
      });
    },
  };
}
--- test
const q = createQueue(2);
let running = 0;
let peak = 0;
const job = (ms, value) => async () => {
  running++;
  peak = Math.max(peak, running);
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
  running--;
  return value;
};
const results = await Promise.all([q.add(job(20, "a")), q.add(job(10, "b")), q.add(job(10, "c"))]);
assertEqual(results, ["a", "b", "c"], "Each promise should resolve with its own task''s result");
assertEqual(peak, 2, "No more than two tasks should run at once");

const failing = createQueue(1);
const boom = failing.add(() => {
  throw new Error("boom");
});
const after = failing.add(() => "still running");
let msg = null;
try {
  await boom;
} catch (err) {
  msg = err.message;
}
assertEqual(msg, "boom", "A failed task should reject its own promise");
assertEqual(await after, "still running", "A failed task must not block the next one");
--- hint
Keep the waiting tasks in an array of `{ task, resolve, reject }` records. `add` returns a `new Promise` whose resolvers go into that array, then asks the queue to start work.
--- hint
Write a `next()` function that starts one waiting task if `active < concurrency`. Run it from `add`, and call it again in `.finally` after decrementing `active`, so each freed slot is refilled.
```

> **Pitfall:** A common mistake is to decrement the counter only on success. A failed task then holds its slot forever, and the queue quietly stops after the first error. Decrement in `finally`, or in both branches, so every outcome frees the slot.

## Stage 3: collect every outcome, in input order

Sometimes the caller doesn''t want the queue at all. It has a fixed list of tasks and wants every outcome, successes and failures alike, in the order the tasks were given. That''s a worker pool: a fixed number of workers pull the next index from a shared counter until the list is empty. Each result goes into its own slot, so the output order is the input order even when tasks finish out of order.

```js run
const wait = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
const tasks = [() => wait(30).then(() => "slow"), () => wait(5).then(() => "fast")];
const finished = await Promise.allSettled(tasks.map((t) => t()));
console.log(finished.map((r) => r.value));
```

`Promise.allSettled` describes each outcome without throwing, and it''s the shape the next challenge returns. Its results are in input order too, regardless of which promise settled first.

```js exercise id=run-all title="A worker pool that reports every outcome"
async function runAll(tasks, limit) {
  // TODO: run the task functions with at most `limit` in flight at once.
  // Resolve with an array, in input order, of { status: "fulfilled", value } or
  // { status: "rejected", reason } for each task. Never stop early on a failure.
  return null;
}
--- solution
async function runAll(tasks, limit) {
  const results = new Array(tasks.length);
  let nextIndex = 0;
  const worker = async () => {
    while (nextIndex < tasks.length) {
      const i = nextIndex++;
      try {
        results[i] = { status: "fulfilled", value: await tasks[i]() };
      } catch (reason) {
        results[i] = { status: "rejected", reason };
      }
    }
  };
  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, tasks.length) }, worker));
  return results;
}
--- test
const wait = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
const outcomes = await runAll([
  () => 1,
  () => {
    throw new Error("no");
  },
  async () => 3,
], 2);
assertEqual(outcomes.map((o) => o.status), ["fulfilled", "rejected", "fulfilled"], "Each slot should report its own status");
assertEqual(outcomes[0].value, 1, "Fulfilled outcomes carry a value");
assertEqual(outcomes[1].reason.message, "no", "Rejected outcomes carry the error as reason");
assertEqual(outcomes[2].value, 3, "A later task should still run after a failure");

const ordered = await runAll([() => wait(30).then(() => "slow"), () => wait(5).then(() => "fast")], 2);
assertEqual(ordered.map((o) => o.value), ["slow", "fast"], "Results should follow input order, not finishing order");
--- hint
Start `limit` workers, each with the same loop: take the next unused index, await that task inside a `try`, and store the outcome at that index.
--- hint
Let each worker share the `nextIndex` counter. The `try`/`catch` inside the loop turns a rejection into a `{ status: "rejected" }` entry instead of stopping the worker.
```

## Stage 4: the integrated `TaskQueue`

Now assemble the pieces into one class, with the features a real queue needs: a `running` count and a `size` of waiting tasks, `pause` and `resume` to hold back new work, and `onIdle` to wait until everything, including the waiting list, has drained.

The design choice that makes the rest easy is a single `#pump` method. It runs whenever something changes (a task is added, a task finishes, the queue resumes), and it starts as many waiting tasks as the limit and the paused flag allow. Every other method only changes state and calls `#pump`.

```js run
class Counter {
  #count = 0;
  #bump() {
    this.#count++;
  }
  hit() {
    this.#bump();
    return this.#count;
  }
}

const counter = new Counter();
counter.hit();
console.log(counter.hit(), counter.count);
```

The class keeps `#count` and `#bump` private, so the only way to change the count is through `hit`. The `counter.count` in the log is `undefined` because no public property exists. The queue''s `#pump` follows the same pattern: callers use `add`, `pause` and `resume`, and the scheduling logic stays behind them.

> **Under the hood:** Private `#` methods and fields are real privacy, enforced by the engine, not a naming convention. Code outside the class can''t call `#pump`, so no caller can start tasks while the queue is paused, and no caller can corrupt the running count.

```js exercise id=task-queue title="The integrated TaskQueue"
class TaskQueue {
  constructor(concurrency) {
    this.concurrency = concurrency;
  }
  // TODO: implement the rest of the class.
  // add(task): queue a task and return a promise for its result. Tasks start in FIFO order,
  //   never more than `concurrency` at once, and each task starts asynchronously.
  // get running: the number of tasks currently running.
  // get size: the number of tasks waiting for a slot.
  // pause(): stop starting new tasks. Running tasks continue.
  // resume(): start new tasks again.
  // onIdle(): return a promise that resolves when nothing is running and nothing is waiting.
  add(task) {
    return null;
  }
}
--- solution
class TaskQueue {
  #concurrency;
  #waiting = [];
  #running = 0;
  #paused = false;
  #idleWaiters = [];

  constructor(concurrency) {
    this.#concurrency = concurrency;
  }
  get running() {
    return this.#running;
  }
  get size() {
    return this.#waiting.length;
  }
  add(task) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      this.#waiting.push({ task, resolve, reject });
      this.#pump();
    });
  }
  pause() {
    this.#paused = true;
  }
  resume() {
    this.#paused = false;
    this.#pump();
  }
  onIdle() {
    if (this.#running === 0 && this.#waiting.length === 0) return Promise.resolve();
    return new Promise((resolve) => this.#idleWaiters.push(resolve));
  }
  #pump() {
    while (!this.#paused && this.#running < this.#concurrency && this.#waiting.length > 0) {
      const { task, resolve, reject } = this.#waiting.shift();
      this.#running++;
      Promise.resolve()
        .then(task)
        .then(resolve, reject)
        .finally(() => {
          this.#running--;
          this.#pump();
          if (this.#running === 0 && this.#waiting.length === 0) {
            for (const w of this.#idleWaiters.splice(0)) w();
          }
        });
    }
  }
}
--- test
const q = new TaskQueue(2);
const order = [];
const job = (name, ms) => async () => {
  order.push("start " + name);
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
  order.push("end " + name);
  return name.toUpperCase();
};
const p1 = q.add(job("a", 30));
const p2 = q.add(job("b", 10));
const p3 = q.add(job("c", 10));
assertEqual(q.running, 2, "Two tasks should be running right after add");
assertEqual(q.size, 1, "The third task should wait for a slot");
assertEqual(await Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]), ["A", "B", "C"], "Each promise should resolve with its task''s result");
assertEqual(order.slice(0, 2), ["start a", "start b"], "The first two tasks should start in FIFO order");
await q.onIdle();
assertEqual(q.running, 0, "Nothing should be running once idle");

q.pause();
let laterDone = false;
const later = q.add(() => "later").then((v) => {
  laterDone = true;
  return v;
});
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 20));
assertEqual(laterDone, false, "A paused queue must not start new tasks");
q.resume();
assertEqual(await later, "later", "Resuming should start the waiting task");

const slow = new TaskQueue(1);
slow.add(() => new Promise((res) => setTimeout(() => res(1), 20)));
let idle = false;
const waitIdle = slow.onIdle().then(() => {
  idle = true;
});
assertEqual(idle, false, "onIdle should wait while a task is running");
await waitIdle;
assertEqual(idle, true, "onIdle should resolve once the queue is empty");

const failing = new TaskQueue(1);
const bad = failing.add(() => {
  throw new Error("bad");
});
const fine = failing.add(() => "fine");
let badMessage = null;
try {
  await bad;
} catch (err) {
  badMessage = err.message;
}
assertEqual(badMessage, "bad", "A failed task should reject its own promise");
assertEqual(await fine, "fine", "A failed task should not block later tasks");
--- hint
Keep the state as private fields: the waiting list, the running count, the paused flag and the idle waiters. `add` pushes a record and calls a private `#pump` method.
--- hint
`#pump` loops while the queue is not paused, has a free slot and has waiting tasks. Inside it, `Promise.resolve().then(task).then(resolve, reject).finally(...)` runs each task. The `finally` decrements `#running`, calls `#pump` again and resolves idle waiters when the queue is empty.
```

## Debugging: the queue that stopped after one failure

Before the final recap, one bug from the real world. A queue that works in every happy-path test can stall the first time a task throws, because the failure path skipped the bookkeeping. Read this version and decide what the next waiting task would see.

```js debug id=stalled-queue title="The queue that stopped after one failure"
// Like createQueue, but a failing task must free its slot so the next task can run.
function createQueue(concurrency) {
  const waiting = [];
  let active = 0;
  const next = () => {
    if (active >= concurrency || waiting.length === 0) return;
    const { task, resolve, reject } = waiting.shift();
    active++;
    Promise.resolve()
      .then(task)
      .then(
        (value) => {
          active--;
          resolve(value);
          next();
        },
        (err) => {
          reject(err);
        },
      );
    next();
  };
  return {
    add(task) {
      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        waiting.push({ task, resolve, reject });
        next();
      });
    },
  };
}
--- solution
function createQueue(concurrency) {
  const waiting = [];
  let active = 0;
  const next = () => {
    if (active >= concurrency || waiting.length === 0) return;
    const { task, resolve, reject } = waiting.shift();
    active++;
    Promise.resolve()
      .then(task)
      .then(
        (value) => {
          active--;
          resolve(value);
          next();
        },
        (err) => {
          active--;
          reject(err);
          next();
        },
      );
    next();
  };
  return {
    add(task) {
      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        waiting.push({ task, resolve, reject });
        next();
      });
    },
  };
}
--- test
const q = createQueue(1);
const bad = q.add(() => {
  throw new Error("bad");
});
const ok = q.add(() => "ok");
let message = null;
try {
  await bad;
} catch (err) {
  message = err.message;
}
assertEqual(message, "bad", "The failure should reach the caller");
const outcome = await Promise.race([ok, new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => r("stalled"), 50))]);
assertEqual(outcome, "ok", "A failed task must free its slot so the next task can run");
--- hint
Compare the success branch with the failure branch. What does the success branch do that the failure branch doesn''t?
--- hint
The failure callback needs the same bookkeeping as the success callback: decrement `active`, reject the caller, then call `next()` to refill the slot.
```

> **Pitfall:** A promise callback that throws, or a branch that forgets a line of bookkeeping, fails silently. The caller is left waiting, and nothing reports an error. Test the failure path of every queue or pool, not just the success path.

## Predict: when does the next task run?

Last, a prediction about microtask ordering, since the queue depends on it. Predict the output before you check the options.

```js predict id=predict-microtask-order title="Two microtasks, one log"
const log = [];
const queueish = (task) => {
  Promise.resolve().then(task);
};
queueish(() => log.push("task"));
log.push("after add");
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(log.join(",")));
--- options
task,after add
after add
* after add,task
task
--- explain
`queueish` schedules `task` as a microtask, but the callback doesn''t run yet. `"after add"` is pushed synchronously. The final `Promise.resolve().then` is also a microtask, scheduled after the task''s, so the task runs first and pushes `"task"`, and then the logging callback prints `after add,task`. Scheduling order decides microtask order, and this is why `add` never runs the task synchronously.
```

## Recap

- Starting work is separate from accepting it. Defer the task with `Promise.resolve().then(...)` so it never runs inside the caller''s call stack.
- A concurrency limit needs two pieces of state: the running count and a FIFO list of waiters. Free the slot on every outcome, including failure.
- Worker pools keep results in input order by writing each outcome to its own index, and they never stop early on a failure.
- A single `#pump` method that every state change calls keeps the queue''s invariants in one place.
- Test the failure path and the pause path, not just the happy path.

**Up next:** the second capstone, a reactive store with subscriptions, the core idea behind modern state libraries.
', 0, '[]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_javascript_advanced_7_2', 'sec_javascript_advanced_7', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'Project: a reactive store with subscriptions', 'interactive', 1, 2400, NULL, NULL, 'Modern state libraries (Redux, Zustand, Svelte stores, the signals behind several frameworks) share one core idea: keep the application state in one place, change it only through a controlled door, and tell interested parts of the program when it changes. Everything else is built on top of that. A component re-renders when the slice it reads changes. A cache invalidates when its source changes. A form validates when a field changes. The machinery is small, and the details decide whether an app stays fast and predictable.

In this capstone you''ll build a reactive store in five steps. You''ll write the basic container, add subscriptions with change detection, select a slice of state so listeners ignore unrelated changes, batch several updates into one notification, and then assemble everything into one store. Each step has its own challenge, and the test for each step checks behavior you can use directly in an app.

## The state container

The first run cell shows the core trick. The state lives in a variable that only the functions can reach, and the public door is a `set` that accepts either a new value or an updater. Updaters are important because they read the latest state at the moment of the update, so two updates in a row can''t clobber each other.

```js run
let count = 0;
const setCount = (next) => {
  count = typeof next === "function" ? next(count) : next;
};

setCount(5);
setCount((c) => c + 1);
console.log(count);
```

Notice what the caller can''t do: reach in and assign `count` directly. Every change goes through one function, and that''s the single place where you can add notifications, logging or validation later.

## Change detection with `Object.is`

Before a store notifies anyone, it has to decide whether anything actually changed. The comparison it should use is `Object.is`, which is `===` with two fixes: `NaN` equals `NaN`, and `+0` is different from `-0`. For a store, the more important point is that objects compare by identity.

```js run
console.log(Object.is(NaN, NaN), NaN === NaN, Object.is(0, -0), 0 === -0);
```

That identity rule has a practical consequence. A selector that returns a fresh object on every call looks like a change every time, even when the data didn''t move:

```js run
const state = { user: { name: "Ada" }, theme: "light" };
const selectUser = (s) => ({ user: s.user });

console.log(Object.is(selectUser(state), selectUser(state)), Object.is(selectUser(state).user, state.user));
```

The wrapper object is new each time, but the `user` inside it is the same reference. Stores rely on stable references: when a state update leaves a branch alone, the branch keeps its identity, and a selector that returns that branch reports no change. This is the structural sharing idea from the immutability lesson, now doing real work.

## Stage 1: a store with `get` and `set`

Your first challenge is the container itself. It must hold the state privately, return it from `get`, and accept updaters in `set`.

```js exercise id=create-store title="A store with get and set"
function createStore(initial) {
  // TODO: return { get, set }. get() returns the current state.
  // set(next) accepts a new state, or a function that receives the current state and returns the new one.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function createStore(initial) {
  let state = initial;
  return {
    get() {
      return state;
    },
    set(next) {
      state = typeof next === "function" ? next(state) : next;
    },
  };
}
--- test
const store = createStore({ count: 0 });
assertEqual(store.get(), { count: 0 }, "get should return the initial state");
store.set({ count: 5 });
assertEqual(store.get(), { count: 5 }, "set should replace the state with a value");
store.set((s) => ({ count: s.count + 1 }));
assertEqual(store.get(), { count: 6 }, "set should accept an updater that receives the current state");
const numbers = createStore(1);
numbers.set((n) => n * 10);
assertEqual(numbers.get(), 10, "Updaters work with primitive states too");
--- hint
Keep the state in a `let` variable inside `createStore`, and return an object whose methods read and write that variable.
--- hint
In `set`, check `typeof next === "function"`. If it is, call it with the current state and store the result. Otherwise store `next` directly.
```

## Stage 2: subscriptions and change notifications

A store that nobody hears from is just a variable. Subscriptions let other code react. The rules are simple, and each one matters: `subscribe` returns an unsubscribe function, listeners receive both the next and previous state, and setting an equal value does nothing at all.

```js run
const listeners = [];
let value = 1;
listeners.push((next, prev) => console.log("changed", prev, "->", next));

const setValue = (next) => {
  const prev = value;
  value = next;
  if (!Object.is(prev, next)) listeners.forEach((fn) => fn(next, prev));
};

setValue(2);
setValue(2);
```

The second `setValue(2)` is silent, which is the behavior you want. A store that notifies on every write forces every subscriber to do its own comparison, and most subscribers get that comparison wrong.

```js exercise id=subscribable-store title="Notify subscribers when the state changes"
function createSubscribableStore(initial) {
  // TODO: return { get, set, subscribe }. Like createStore, but:
  // subscribe(listener) registers a listener and returns an unsubscribe function.
  // After a set that changes the state (compared with Object.is), every listener is called
  // as listener(nextState, previousState). Setting an equal state does nothing.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function createSubscribableStore(initial) {
  let state = initial;
  const listeners = new Set();
  return {
    get() {
      return state;
    },
    set(next) {
      const prev = state;
      state = typeof next === "function" ? next(state) : next;
      if (Object.is(state, prev)) return;
      for (const fn of [...listeners]) fn(state, prev);
    },
    subscribe(fn) {
      listeners.add(fn);
      return () => {
        listeners.delete(fn);
      };
    },
  };
}
--- test
const store = createSubscribableStore(0);
const log = [];
const stop = store.subscribe((next, prev) => log.push([next, prev]));
store.set(1);
store.set(1);
store.set((n) => n + 1);
assertEqual(log, [[1, 0], [2, 1]], "Listeners should receive (next, previous), and equal values should not notify");
stop();
store.set(10);
assertEqual(log.length, 2, "An unsubscribed listener should stop receiving changes");
assertEqual(store.get(), 10, "The state should still update without subscribers");
--- hint
Keep a `Set` of listeners next to the state. `subscribe` adds to the set, and returns a function that deletes from it.
--- hint
In `set`, remember the previous state and compute the next one. Return early if `Object.is(state, prev)`. Otherwise loop over a copy of the listeners and call each with `(state, prev)`.
```

> **Pitfall:** Iterate over a copy of the listener set (`[...listeners]`). If a listener unsubscribes itself while the loop runs, a live iteration can skip the next listener, which is exactly the bug from the design patterns lesson.

## Stage 3: select a slice

Most components don''t care about the whole state. A header wants `user.name`, and a counter wants `count`. A selector is a function that picks out the slice a listener cares about, and `select` calls the listener only when that slice changes. An update to the theme should never wake a listener that reads the count.

The `select` function below works on any store with `get` and `subscribe`, which is why its test builds a tiny store by hand. That''s the point of keeping the interface small.

```js exercise id=select-slice title="Subscribe to one slice of the state"
function select(store, selector, listener) {
  // TODO: subscribe to `store`, and call listener(value, previousValue) only when
  // selector(state) changes (compared with Object.is). Return the unsubscribe function.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function select(store, selector, listener) {
  let last = selector(store.get());
  return store.subscribe((state) => {
    const value = selector(state);
    if (Object.is(value, last)) return;
    const prev = last;
    last = value;
    listener(value, prev);
  });
}
--- test
let state = { count: 0, theme: "light" };
const subs = new Set();
const store = {
  get: () => state,
  subscribe(fn) {
    subs.add(fn);
    return () => subs.delete(fn);
  },
  set(next) {
    const prev = state;
    state = next;
    for (const fn of [...subs]) fn(state, prev);
  },
};
const seen = [];
const stop = select(store, (s) => s.count, (value, prev) => seen.push([value, prev]));
store.set({ count: 0, theme: "dark" });
assertEqual(seen, [], "A change to another slice should not notify");
store.set({ count: 3, theme: "dark" });
assertEqual(seen, [[3, 0]], "A changed slice should notify with the new and previous values");
stop();
store.set({ count: 4, theme: "dark" });
assertEqual(seen.length, 1, "After unsubscribing, the listener should stop");
--- hint
Compute the slice once when you subscribe and keep it in a variable. Each time the store notifies, compute the slice again and compare it with that variable.
--- hint
Inside the subscription callback: `const value = selector(state)`. If `Object.is(value, last)`, return. Otherwise update `last` and call `listener(value, prev)`.
```

## Stage 4: batching, and a read that sees the draft

Sometimes one user action updates several fields, and subscribers should hear about the result once, not once per field. A batch collects updates in a draft and writes the store a single time at the end. The draft has to behave like the store while the batch runs: a read inside the batch must see the updates that already happened in it.

Here''s a batch helper with a bug in its draft. Read it closely before you run the test.

```js debug id=batch-stale-draft title="Updates that overwrite each other"
// Run several updates as one: each read and update sees the batch''s earlier updates,
// and the store is written once at the end, if anything changed.
function runBatch(store, fn) {
  const start = store.get();
  let pending = start;
  fn({
    get: () => start,
    set(next) {
      pending = typeof next === "function" ? next(pending) : next;
    },
  });
  if (pending !== start) store.set(pending);
}
--- solution
function runBatch(store, fn) {
  const start = store.get();
  let pending = start;
  fn({
    get: () => pending,
    set(next) {
      pending = typeof next === "function" ? next(pending) : next;
    },
  });
  if (pending !== start) store.set(pending);
}
--- test
let value = { a: 0 };
const store = { get: () => value, set(next) { value = next; } };
runBatch(store, (draft) => {
  draft.set({ a: draft.get().a + 1 });
  draft.set({ a: draft.get().a + 1 });
});
assertEqual(value, { a: 2 }, "A read inside the batch should see the earlier updates in that batch");

let writes = 0;
const counted = { get: () => value, set(next) { writes++; value = next; } };
runBatch(counted, (draft) => {
  draft.set({ a: 5 });
  draft.set({ a: 6 });
});
assertEqual(writes, 1, "The store should be written once per batch");
assertEqual(value, { a: 6 }, "The final value should be the last update");
--- hint
Look at what `get` returns while the batch is still running. Should it return the store''s value from before the batch, or the draft?
--- hint
`get` should return `pending`, the draft that already includes the earlier updates in this batch.
```

> **Under the hood:** A batch works because the draft is a separate value. Reads and writes go to `pending`, and the store is touched once. Libraries call this a transaction or a draft, and immer''s `produce` is the same idea applied to nested objects.

## Final: the reactive store

Now assemble the store. It needs `get` and `set` from stage 1, `subscribe` with change detection from stage 2, `select` from stage 3, and batching from stage 4, combined so that a batch suppresses notifications until it finishes.

The design is one `depth` counter and one `notify` helper. `notify` returns early while a batch is open, and `batch` increments the counter, runs its function, decrements it in a `finally` and then notifies once with the state from before the batch. `select` is `subscribe` plus a remembered value, so it reuses the notification path rather than duplicating it.

```js exercise id=create-reactive-store title="A reactive store with select and batch"
function createReactiveStore(initial) {
  // TODO: return a store with:
  // get(): the current state
  // set(valueOrUpdater): update the state and notify subscribers if it changed (Object.is)
  // subscribe(listener): listener(next, prev) on each change; returns an unsubscribe function
  // select(selector, listener): like subscribe, but only when selector(state) changes
  // batch(fn): run fn. Updates inside it notify subscribers once, after fn returns, if the state changed
  return null;
}
--- solution
function createReactiveStore(initial) {
  let state = initial;
  let depth = 0;
  const listeners = new Set();
  const notify = (prev) => {
    if (depth > 0 || Object.is(state, prev)) return;
    for (const fn of [...listeners]) fn(state, prev);
  };
  const store = {
    get: () => state,
    set(next) {
      const prev = state;
      state = typeof next === "function" ? next(state) : next;
      notify(prev);
    },
    subscribe(fn) {
      listeners.add(fn);
      return () => listeners.delete(fn);
    },
    select(selector, listener) {
      let last = selector(state);
      return store.subscribe((next) => {
        const value = selector(next);
        if (Object.is(value, last)) return;
        const prev = last;
        last = value;
        listener(value, prev);
      });
    },
    batch(fn) {
      const prev = state;
      depth++;
      try {
        fn();
      } finally {
        depth--;
        notify(prev);
      }
    },
  };
  return store;
}
--- test
const s = createReactiveStore({ n: 0, label: "x" });
const seen = [];
const unsub = s.subscribe((next) => seen.push(next.n));
s.set((st) => ({ ...st, n: 1 }));
assertEqual(seen, [1], "Subscribers should receive the new state once");
s.set((st) => st);
assertEqual(seen, [1], "Setting the same state should not notify");
unsub();
s.set({ n: 9, label: "y" });
assertEqual(seen, [1], "Unsubscribed listeners should stop receiving changes");

const t = createReactiveStore({ count: 0, theme: "light" });
const changes = [];
t.select((st) => st.count, (value, prev) => changes.push([value, prev]));
t.set((st) => ({ ...st, theme: "dark" }));
assertEqual(changes, [], "A change outside the selected slice should not notify");
t.set((st) => ({ ...st, count: 2 }));
assertEqual(changes, [[2, 0]], "A changed slice should notify with the new and previous values");

const b = createReactiveStore({ a: 1, b: 1 });
let notifications = 0;
b.subscribe(() => notifications++);
b.batch(() => {
  b.set((st) => ({ ...st, a: 2 }));
  b.set((st) => ({ ...st, b: 2 }));
});
assertEqual(notifications, 1, "A batch should notify once, after all its updates");
assertEqual(b.get(), { a: 2, b: 2 }, "Every update in the batch should be applied");
b.batch(() => {});
assertEqual(notifications, 1, "An empty batch should not notify");
--- hint
Start from the stage 2 store: a `Set` of listeners, a `notify(prev)` helper, and `set` calling it. Then add a `depth` counter that `notify` checks.
--- hint
`select` can be built on `subscribe`: remember the last selected value and compare on each change. `batch` increments `depth`, runs `fn` inside a `try`, and in the `finally` decrements `depth` and calls `notify` with the state from before the batch.
```

## Predict: unsubscribing is per listener

Last, a prediction about subscriptions. Predict the output before you check the options. The subscriber list is copied before each notification, which is the same snapshot discipline you''ve been using.

```js predict id=predict-store-notify title="Who hears the change"
const seen = [];
const subs = [];
const subscribe = (fn) => {
  subs.push(fn);
  return () => subs.splice(subs.indexOf(fn), 1);
};
const offA = subscribe((v) => seen.push("a" + v));
subscribe((v) => seen.push("b" + v));
subs.slice().forEach((fn) => fn(1));
offA();
subs.slice().forEach((fn) => fn(2));
console.log(seen.join(" "));
--- options
a1 b1 a2 b2
* a1 b1 b2
a1 a2 b2
b1 b2
--- explain
The first notification reaches both listeners, so `seen` gets `a1` and `b1`. `offA()` removes exactly the listener it returned for, which is `a`, leaving `b` subscribed. The second notification reaches only `b`, so the output is `a1 b1 b2`. A common bug is an unsubscribe that removes by position or pops the last listener, which would remove the wrong one.
```

## Recap

- A store owns its state. Changes go through `set`, which accepts a value or an updater that reads the latest state.
- Notify only on real changes, compared with `Object.is`, and pass both the next and previous state to listeners.
- Selectors should return stable references. A new object on every call looks like a change every time.
- `select` subscribes to a slice, so unrelated updates don''t wake a listener.
- A batch uses a draft for reads and writes, writes the store once, and notifies once, even when the batch throws.

**Up next:** the final capstone, an API client with retries, caching and cancellation, built stage by stage on the same habits you''ve practiced here.
', 0, '[]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_javascript_advanced_7_3', 'sec_javascript_advanced_7', 'crs_javascript_advanced', 'Project: an API client with retries, caching and cancellation', 'interactive', 2, 2640, NULL, NULL, 'Every application ends up talking to servers, and every server eventually has a bad afternoon. Requests fail with a dropped connection, return a 503 during a deploy, or take ten seconds when the user has already navigated away. A good API client handles those moments without making the app worse: it retries the failures that are worth retrying, reuses answers it already has, shares work that''s already in flight, and stops waiting when nobody needs the result anymore.

In this final capstone you''ll build those behaviors one stage at a time: classifying and reporting HTTP failures, retrying with exponential backoff, caching with a clock you control, and cancelling with `AbortController`. Then you''ll compose all four into one `createApiClient`. There''s no network in this course, so the client takes a `transport` function as an argument. In an app that would wrap `fetch`, and in tests it''s a fake that you script. Keeping the transport injected is the same dependency-injection habit from the testing lesson, and it''s what makes every behavior here deterministic.

## The transport contract

A transport is an async function from a path to `{ status, body }`. A successful HTTP exchange resolves with its status. A network failure, such as a dropped connection, rejects instead. The client has to treat those two kinds of failure differently, and the first challenge is about making that distinction visible in the error.

```js run
const fakeTransport = async (path) =>
  path === "/ok" ? { status: 200, body: { hello: "world" } } : { status: 404, body: null };

const res = await fakeTransport("/ok");
console.log(res.status, res.body);
```

## Stage 1: turn HTTP status into a usable error

The client should return the body for a 2xx response and throw for everything else. The thrown error needs a `status` property, because retry and display logic both need to know what happened. Attaching the status to an `Error` with `Object.assign` keeps the message readable and the data machine-friendly.

```js exercise id=fetch-json title="Return the body, or throw with the status"
async function fetchJson(transport, path) {
  // TODO: call transport(path). Return res.body for a 2xx status.
  // For any other status, throw an Error whose message includes the status
  // and which has a numeric `status` property.
  return null;
}
--- solution
async function fetchJson(transport, path) {
  const res = await transport(path);
  if (res.status < 200 || res.status >= 300) {
    throw Object.assign(new Error(`Request failed with ${res.status}`), { status: res.status });
  }
  return res.body;
}
--- test
const ok = async (path) => ({ status: 200, body: { path } });
assertEqual(await fetchJson(ok, "/users"), { path: "/users" }, "A 2xx response should return its body");
const missing = async () => ({ status: 404, body: null });
let caught = null;
try {
  await fetchJson(missing, "/nope");
} catch (err) {
  caught = err;
}
assert(caught instanceof Error, "A 404 should throw an Error");
assertEqual(caught.status, 404, "The error should carry the HTTP status");
assert(caught.message.includes("404"), "The message should mention the status");
--- hint
Check the range first: `res.status < 200 || res.status >= 300` means the request failed. Return `res.body` in the other case.
--- hint
Build the error with `Object.assign(new Error(message), { status: res.status })`, and throw it.
```

## Stage 2: retry transient failures with backoff

Retrying is only safe for failures that might succeed later. A network error and a 5xx response are transient. A 404 or 400 will fail the same way every time, so retrying it wastes time and can hammer a server that''s already unhappy. Retries also need spacing: waiting a little longer after each failure (exponential backoff) gives a struggling server room to recover.

The helper below takes a function that receives the attempt number, retries the transient cases, and throws the last error when it runs out of attempts.

Here is the shape of a retry loop with a fake operation that fails once before it succeeds:

```js run
const attempts = [];
const flakyOnce = async () => {
  attempts.push(attempts.length);
  if (attempts.length < 2) throw new Error("transient");
  return "recovered";
};

let result = null;
for (let i = 0; i < 3 && result === null; i++) {
  try {
    result = await flakyOnce();
  } catch {
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 5));
  }
}
console.log(result, attempts.length);
```

The loop is the core of every retry helper. Each failed attempt waits before the next one, and the loop stops at a fixed budget so a permanent failure can''t spin forever.

```js exercise id=with-retry title="Retry transient failures with exponential backoff"
async function withRetry(fn, { retries = 2, baseDelay = 5 } = {}) {
  // TODO: call fn(attempt), starting at attempt 0. Return its result.
  // If it throws a transient failure (no status, or status 500 or higher), wait
  // baseDelay * 2 ** attempt milliseconds and try again, up to `retries` retries.
  // Errors with a 4xx status are thrown immediately. When retries run out, throw the last error.
  return null;
}
--- solution
async function withRetry(fn, { retries = 2, baseDelay = 5 } = {}) {
  for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await fn(attempt);
    } catch (err) {
      const transient = err.status === undefined || err.status >= 500;
      if (!transient || attempt >= retries) throw err;
      await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, baseDelay * 2 ** attempt));
    }
  }
}
--- test
let calls = 0;
const flaky = async () => {
  calls++;
  if (calls < 3) throw Object.assign(new Error("503"), { status: 503 });
  return "ok";
};
assertEqual(await withRetry(flaky, { baseDelay: 1 }), "ok", "A flaky call should succeed on a later attempt");
assertEqual(calls, 3, "It should keep trying until the call succeeds");

let notFound = 0;
const missing = async () => {
  notFound++;
  throw Object.assign(new Error("404"), { status: 404 });
};
let status = null;
try {
  await withRetry(missing, { baseDelay: 1 });
} catch (err) {
  status = err.status;
}
assertEqual(status, 404, "A 4xx error should be thrown");
assertEqual(notFound, 1, "A 4xx error should never be retried");

let down = 0;
const broken = async () => {
  down++;
  throw Object.assign(new Error("down"), { status: 500 });
};
let last = null;
try {
  await withRetry(broken, { retries: 2, baseDelay: 1 });
} catch (err) {
  last = err.message;
}
assertEqual(down, 3, "One try plus two retries, then give up");
assertEqual(last, "down", "The last error should be thrown");
--- hint
A `for` loop with a `try` inside can `return` the result on success. In the `catch`, decide whether the error is transient, and if it is not, throw it.
--- hint
Wait with `await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, baseDelay * 2 ** attempt))`. Give up when `attempt >= retries`.
```

> **Pitfall:** Retrying a non-idempotent request (a POST that charges a card, for example) can repeat its side effect. Retry reads freely, and retry writes only when the server supports idempotency keys.

## Stage 3: cache with an injected clock

A cache with a time-to-live needs to know the current time, and calling `Date.now()` inside it would make the tests depend on the real clock. Injecting `now` solves that. The cache also needs one more guarantee: if ten components ask for the same resource at the same moment, the loader should run once, and all ten should wait on the same promise. Failed loads must never be cached, or one bad response would poison the cache for the whole TTL.

A cache entry needs a timestamp and a freshness check against the current time. Passing the clock in as a function means a test can move time forward on purpose:

```js run
let clock = 0;
const cache = new Map();
const remember = (key, value) => cache.set(key, { value, at: clock });
const recall = (key, ttl) => {
  const hit = cache.get(key);
  return hit && clock - hit.at < ttl ? hit.value : undefined;
};

remember("user", "Ada");
clock = 50;
console.log(recall("user", 100));
clock = 150;
console.log(recall("user", 100));
```

The first read is fresh, and the second one falls outside the 100 ms window, so the cache reports a miss. The exercise below adds the loader and the sharing rules on top of this check.

```js exercise id=cached-getter title="A cached getter with a TTL and shared loads"
function createCachedGetter(loader, { ttl = 1000, now = () => 0 } = {}) {
  // TODO: return get(key). It returns a promise for loader(key), with these rules:
  // 1. A value loaded less than `ttl` ms ago (by now()) is returned without calling loader.
  // 2. Concurrent calls for the same key share one loader call.
  // 3. A failed load rejects the caller and is not cached.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function createCachedGetter(loader, { ttl = 1000, now = () => 0 } = {}) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inflight = new Map();
  return (key) => {
    const hit = cache.get(key);
    if (hit && now() - hit.at < ttl) return Promise.resolve(hit.value);
    if (!inflight.has(key)) {
      inflight.set(
        key,
        loader(key).then(
          (value) => {
            cache.set(key, { value, at: now() });
            inflight.delete(key);
            return value;
          },
          (err) => {
            inflight.delete(key);
            throw err;
          },
        ),
      );
    }
    return inflight.get(key);
  };
}
--- test
let clock = 0;
let loads = 0;
const get = createCachedGetter(
  async (k) => {
    loads++;
    return k.toUpperCase();
  },
  { ttl: 100, now: () => clock },
);
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([get("x"), get("x")]);
assertEqual([a, b], ["X", "X"], "Concurrent callers should get the same value");
assertEqual(loads, 1, "Concurrent requests for one key should share a single load");
assertEqual(await get("x"), "X", "A fresh cache entry should be returned");
assertEqual(loads, 1, "A cache hit should not load again");
clock = 150;
await get("x");
assertEqual(loads, 2, "After the TTL, the value should be loaded again");

let failNext = true;
const shaky = createCachedGetter(async () => {
  if (failNext) throw new Error("offline");
  return "back";
});
let message = null;
try {
  await shaky("k");
} catch (err) {
  message = err.message;
}
failNext = false;
assertEqual(message, "offline", "A failed load should reject the caller");
assertEqual(await shaky("k"), "back", "A failed load should not be cached");
--- hint
Keep two maps: one for finished values (with the time they were stored) and one for promises that are still loading. Check the cache first, then the in-flight map, and only then call the loader.
--- hint
Store the loader''s promise in `inflight` right away, and in its `then` callbacks write the value to `cache` and delete the in-flight entry on success or failure.
```

> **Under the hood:** Storing the promise, not the value, is what makes concurrent requests share one load. The second caller finds the same pending promise in the map, so it waits on the work already in progress instead of starting a duplicate.

## Stage 4: cancel work nobody is waiting for

A user who navigates away should not keep a request alive in the UI layer. `AbortController` is the standard tool: it produces a `signal`, and when you call `abort()`, every listener on the signal runs. The client''s job is to honor the signal by rejecting early, without calling a task that has already been cancelled.

```js run
const controller = new AbortController();
console.log(controller.signal.aborted);
controller.abort();
console.log(controller.signal.aborted, controller.signal.reason.name);
```

The helper below turns a signal into a rejection. Cancellation doesn''t stop the underlying task (JavaScript promises can''t be stopped), so it only decides whether the caller still gets the result.

```js exercise id=run-cancellable title="Reject early when the signal aborts"
function runCancellable(task, signal) {
  // TODO: return a promise for task(). Rules:
  // - If signal is already aborted, reject with an Error named "AbortError" and do not call task.
  // - If the signal aborts while the task runs, reject with an AbortError right away.
  // - Otherwise resolve or reject with the task''s own outcome.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function runCancellable(task, signal) {
  const abortError = () => Object.assign(new Error("Aborted"), { name: "AbortError" });
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) return reject(abortError());
    const onAbort = () => reject(abortError());
    signal?.addEventListener("abort", onAbort, { once: true });
    Promise.resolve()
      .then(task)
      .then(resolve, reject)
      .finally(() => signal?.removeEventListener("abort", onAbort));
  });
}
--- test
let ran = false;
const pre = new AbortController();
pre.abort();
let preName = null;
try {
  await runCancellable(() => {
    ran = true;
    return 1;
  }, pre.signal);
} catch (err) {
  preName = err.name;
}
assertEqual(preName, "AbortError", "An already aborted signal should reject with AbortError");
assertEqual(ran, false, "The task should not start when the signal is already aborted");

const ctrl = new AbortController();
const slow = runCancellable(() => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => r("done"), 50)), ctrl.signal);
setTimeout(() => ctrl.abort(), 10);
let lateName = null;
try {
  await slow;
} catch (err) {
  lateName = err.name;
}
assertEqual(lateName, "AbortError", "Aborting while the task runs should reject early");
assertEqual(await runCancellable(async () => "fine", new AbortController().signal), "fine", "Without an abort, the result should pass through");
--- hint
Check `signal?.aborted` before you start the task. If the signal is still live, add an `abort` listener that rejects the promise.
--- hint
`signal.addEventListener("abort", onAbort, { once: true })` registers the listener. Remove it in `finally` so finished requests don''t keep listeners alive.
```

> **Pitfall:** Forgetting to remove `abort` listeners is a quiet memory leak. A long-lived signal, such as one tied to the whole page, keeps every finished request''s callback alive until it''s removed.

## Predict: the exponent is right-associative

Retry schedules are often written as powers, which brings in one operator rule you have to know cold. Predict this before you check the options.

```js predict id=predict-backoff-math title="Backoff delays and a surprising exponent"
const delays = [0, 1, 2, 3].map((attempt) => 100 * 2 ** attempt);
console.log(delays.join(" "), 2 ** 3 ** 2);
--- options
100 200 400 800 64
100 200 400 800 8
200 400 800 1600 512
* 100 200 400 800 512
--- explain
The first part maps each attempt to `100 * 2 ** attempt`, giving 100, 200, 400 and 800. The second part looks like it should be `(2 ** 3) ** 2`, which is 64, but `**` is right-associative, so `3 ** 2` is evaluated first and the expression becomes `2 ** 9`, which is 512. Wrap the parts in parentheses whenever you want a specific grouping, especially in backoff formulas.
```

## Final: compose the client

Now combine the stages into `createApiClient`. Its `get` method has to do all of this, in a specific order:

1. Refuse immediately if the signal is already aborted.
2. Return a fresh cached body if one exists.
3. Share one in-flight load per path, where the load itself retries transient failures and throws the status errors from stage 1.
4. Stop waiting as soon as the signal aborts, and don''t cache results that arrive after the caller has gone.

The structure is the same as the stage functions, arranged so each one has one job. The final solution keeps the load loop self-contained, because the integrated challenge runs in a fresh scope.

```js exercise id=create-api-client title="An API client with retries, caching and cancellation"
function createApiClient({ transport, retries = 2, baseDelay = 5, ttl = 1000, now = () => 0 }) {
  // TODO: return { get(path, { signal }) }. Rules:
  // 1. An aborted signal rejects with an AbortError before anything else happens.
  // 2. A body cached less than ttl ms ago (by now()) is returned without calling transport.
  // 3. Concurrent calls for the same path share one in-flight load.
  // 4. Network failures (transport rejects) and 5xx responses are retried with exponential backoff.
  // 5. Other non-2xx responses, and retries that run out, throw an Error with a numeric `status`.
  // 6. Aborting while a load is in flight rejects that caller with an AbortError.
  return null;
}
--- solution
function createApiClient({ transport, retries = 2, baseDelay = 5, ttl = 1000, now = () => 0 }) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inflight = new Map();
  const abortError = () => Object.assign(new Error("Aborted"), { name: "AbortError" });
  const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
  const load = async (path) => {
    for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
      let res;
      try {
        res = await transport(path);
      } catch {
        res = { status: 0, body: null };
      }
      if (res.status >= 200 && res.status < 300) return res.body;
      const transient = res.status === 0 || res.status >= 500;
      if (!transient || attempt >= retries) {
        throw Object.assign(new Error(`Request failed with ${res.status}`), { status: res.status });
      }
      await sleep(baseDelay * 2 ** attempt);
    }
  };
  const abortable = (promise, signal) =>
    new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const onAbort = () => reject(abortError());
      signal?.addEventListener("abort", onAbort, { once: true });
      promise.then(resolve, reject).finally(() => signal?.removeEventListener("abort", onAbort));
    });
  return {
    async get(path, { signal } = {}) {
      if (signal?.aborted) throw abortError();
      const hit = cache.get(path);
      if (hit && now() - hit.at < ttl) return hit.body;
      if (!inflight.has(path)) {
        inflight.set(path, load(path).finally(() => inflight.delete(path)));
      }
      const body = await abortable(inflight.get(path), signal);
      cache.set(path, { body, at: now() });
      return body;
    },
  };
}
--- test
let clock = 0;
const seen = { count: 0 };
let failures = 2;
const transport = async (path) => {
  seen.count++;
  if (path === "/down") return { status: 404, body: null };
  if (path === "/flaky" && failures > 0) {
    failures--;
    throw new Error("network");
  }
  return { status: 200, body: `body of ${path}` };
};
const api = createApiClient({ transport, retries: 2, baseDelay: 1, ttl: 100, now: () => clock });

assertEqual(await api.get("/flaky"), "body of /flaky", "Network failures should be retried until success");
assertEqual(seen.count, 3, "Two failures plus one success should be three transport calls");

const beforeHit = seen.count;
await api.get("/flaky");
assertEqual(seen.count, beforeHit, "A fresh cached body should not call the transport");
clock = 500;
await api.get("/flaky");
assertEqual(seen.count, beforeHit + 1, "An expired entry should be fetched again");

const beforeShared = seen.count;
const [x, y] = await Promise.all([api.get("/other"), api.get("/other")]);
assertEqual(x, "body of /other", "Shared loads should return the body");
assertEqual(y, x, "Both callers should get the same result");
assertEqual(seen.count - beforeShared, 1, "Concurrent requests for one path should share one transport call");

let downStatus = null;
try {
  await api.get("/down");
} catch (err) {
  downStatus = err.status;
}
assertEqual(downStatus, 404, "A 4xx response should throw with its status");

const ctrl = new AbortController();
ctrl.abort();
let abortName = null;
try {
  await api.get("/other", { signal: ctrl.signal });
} catch (err) {
  abortName = err.name;
}
assertEqual(abortName, "AbortError", "An aborted signal should reject with AbortError");

let brokenCalls = 0;
const broken = createApiClient({
  transport: async () => {
    brokenCalls++;
    return { status: 503, body: null };
  },
  retries: 1,
  baseDelay: 1,
});
let brokenStatus = null;
try {
  await broken.get("/x");
} catch (err) {
  brokenStatus = err.status;
}
assertEqual(brokenStatus, 503, "Exhausted retries should throw with the last status");
assertEqual(brokenCalls, 2, "One try plus one retry");
--- hint
Keep three things: a `cache` Map of finished bodies with their timestamps, an `inflight` Map of pending loads, and a `load(path)` loop that retries transient failures with `sleep(baseDelay * 2 ** attempt)`.
--- hint
In `get`, check the signal, then the cache, then start a load only if `inflight` has none. Race the shared promise against the signal with a small helper that rejects on `abort`, and store the body in `cache` once it arrives.
```

## Debugging: the cache that never expires

The final challenge is a debugging exercise on the stage 3 cache. The bug is a sign error in the age check, and it''s the kind that passes every test where the clock never moves.

```js debug id=cache-expiry title="A cache that never expires"
function createCachedGetter(loader, { ttl = 1000, now = () => 0 } = {}) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inflight = new Map();
  return (key) => {
    const hit = cache.get(key);
    if (hit && hit.at - now() < ttl) return Promise.resolve(hit.value);
    if (!inflight.has(key)) {
      inflight.set(
        key,
        loader(key).then(
          (value) => {
            cache.set(key, { value, at: now() });
            inflight.delete(key);
            return value;
          },
          (err) => {
            inflight.delete(key);
            throw err;
          },
        ),
      );
    }
    return inflight.get(key);
  };
}
--- solution
function createCachedGetter(loader, { ttl = 1000, now = () => 0 } = {}) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inflight = new Map();
  return (key) => {
    const hit = cache.get(key);
    if (hit && now() - hit.at < ttl) return Promise.resolve(hit.value);
    if (!inflight.has(key)) {
      inflight.set(
        key,
        loader(key).then(
          (value) => {
            cache.set(key, { value, at: now() });
            inflight.delete(key);
            return value;
          },
          (err) => {
            inflight.delete(key);
            throw err;
          },
        ),
      );
    }
    return inflight.get(key);
  };
}
--- test
let clock = 0;
let loads = 0;
const get = createCachedGetter(
  async () => {
    loads++;
    return loads;
  },
  { ttl: 100, now: () => clock },
);
await get("k");
await get("k");
assertEqual(loads, 1, "A fresh entry should be served from the cache");
clock = 100;
const stale = await get("k");
assertEqual(loads, 2, "Once the TTL has passed, the loader should run again");
assertEqual(stale, 2, "The new value should come from the second load");
--- hint
Read the age check out loud. How much time has passed since the entry was stored? Which number should be subtracted from which?
--- hint
The age is `now() - hit.at`. The check is only fresh while that age is less than `ttl`. The current code subtracts the other way around, so the age is always negative.
```

## Recap

- Treat a network failure and an HTTP status as different events, and attach the status to the error so the rest of the code can act on it.
- Retry only transient failures, and space the attempts with exponential backoff.
- Inject `now` and the transport, so cache expiry and retries stay deterministic in tests.
- Share in-flight work by storing the promise, and never cache a failed load.
- Cancellation rejects the caller early through an `abort` listener. It can''t stop the underlying work, so clean up the listener.
- Use parentheses when you write exponent formulas, because `**` groups to the right.

**Up next:** the course is complete. Take the final exam to check how well the advanced ideas hold together.
