('les_java_basics_1_4', 'sec_java_basics_1', 'crs_java_basics', 'Checkpoint: getting started', 'quiz', 3, 420, NULL, NULL, 'Answer all questions — you need **70%** to pass. You can retake the quiz as many times as you like.', 0, '[{"id":"q1","prompt":"What does `System.out.println(\"Java\")` display?","options":["`\"Java\"` with the quotation marks","`Java` without the quotation marks","`System.out.println`","Nothing until you call `print`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The quotes mark the text in the source code. `println` prints the characters between them, not the quotes themselves."},{"id":"q2","prompt":"A Java cell is missing a semicolon at the end of one statement. What happens when you run it?","options":["The earlier statements run, then the bad line is skipped","Only the bad line is skipped and the rest runs","The whole cell fails to compile and nothing runs","Java adds the semicolon automatically"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Java is compiled before it runs. A syntax error anywhere in the cell prevents the whole cell from compiling, so no statement executes."},{"id":"q3","prompt":"What is the difference between `print` and `println`?","options":["`print` adds a newline; `println` does not","`print` only accepts strings; `println` accepts any type","`println` adds a newline after the value; `print` does not","They are identical in Java"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Both write a value, but `println` moves to the next line afterwards. `print` leaves the cursor on the same line."},{"id":"q4","prompt":"Which declaration correctly stores the text `hello`?","options":["`String greeting = \"hello\";`","`string greeting = ''hello'';`","`String greeting = ''hello'';`","`str greeting = \"hello\";`"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"`String` is capitalised, and a string uses double quotes. Single quotes are for a single `char`."},{"id":"q5","prompt":"What does `System.out.println(7 / 2)` display?","options":["`3.5`","`3`","`4`","A compile error"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Both operands are `int`, so Java performs integer division and discards the remainder. Use `7 / 2.0` for `3.5`."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"What does `System.out.println(\"Total: \" + 4 + 3)` display?","options":["`Total: 7`","`Total: 43`","`7`","`Total: 4 3`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Evaluation goes left to right. `\"Total: \" + 4` becomes the string `\"Total: 4\"`, and adding `3` joins another character, giving `Total: 43`."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"Which value can be stored in a `boolean` variable?","options":["`\"true\"`","`1`","`true`","`''true''`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A `boolean` holds the literal `true` or `false`, without quotes. The quoted version is a `String`, `1` is an `int`, and `''true''` is not a valid `char`."},{"id":"q8","prompt":"How do you declare a variable that holds a single letter `A`?","options":["`char letter = \"A\";`","`char letter = ''A'';`","`String letter = ''A'';`","`letter char = ''A'';`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A `char` uses single quotes around exactly one character. Double quotes would make a `String`."},{"id":"q9","prompt":"Which variable name is valid Java?","options":["`2ndPlace`","`total-price`","`totalPrice`","`class`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Names cannot start with a digit, cannot contain a hyphen, and cannot be a reserved word such as `class`. `totalPrice` follows camelCase convention."},{"id":"q10","prompt":"What happens if you try to assign a new value to a variable declared `final`?","options":["The value changes, but a warning is printed","The assignment is ignored","The code does not compile","The variable becomes a `String`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`final` means the variable can be assigned once. A later assignment is a compile-time error, which is useful for constants."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_java_basics_2_1', 'sec_java_basics_2', 'crs_java_basics', 'Numbers and arithmetic', 'interactive', 0, 960, NULL, NULL, 'Java''s number types are precise about what they can hold. `int` is a 32-bit whole number, `long` is 64-bit, and `double` stores decimals in the same approximate way most languages do. Choosing the right type prevents a surprising amount of trouble.

## Arithmetic and precedence

```java run
int a = 17;
int b = 5;

System.out.println(a + b);
System.out.println(a - b);
System.out.println(a * b);
System.out.println(a / b);
System.out.println(a % b);
```

`*`, `/` and `%` happen before `+` and `-`, just like in maths. Use parentheses when you want a different order:

```java run
System.out.println(2 + 3 * 4);
System.out.println((2 + 3) * 4);
System.out.println(10 - 4 - 3);
System.out.println(10 - (4 - 3));
```

The first prints `14`, the second `20`. Subtraction is left-associative, so the third prints `3`; the parentheses in the fourth change it to `9`.

## Integer division and remainders

With two `int` values, `/` throws away the fraction. `%` gives what is left over:

```java run
int totalSeconds = 367;
int minutes = totalSeconds / 60;
int seconds = totalSeconds % 60;
System.out.println(minutes + " minutes and " + seconds + " seconds");
```

This "divide and remainder" pair appears everywhere: converting currencies, splitting work into pages, and pulling digits out of a number.

## The Math class

`Math` is a utility class with common operations. It is already available, so no import is needed for these calls:

```java run
System.out.println(Math.abs(-7));
System.out.println(Math.max(12, 30));
System.out.println(Math.min(12, 30));
System.out.println(Math.pow(2, 10));
System.out.println(Math.sqrt(81));
System.out.println(Math.round(3.6));
```

`Math.pow` returns a `double`, even when both arguments are whole numbers. `Math.round` returns a `long`.

## Converting between number types

A value can widen automatically when there is no loss, for example `int` to `double`. Going the other way needs a **cast**, and a cast truncates:

```java run
int whole = 42;
double widened = whole;
System.out.println(widened);

double price = 19.99;
int truncated = (int) price;
System.out.println(truncated);

int count = 10;
count += 5;
count *= 2;
System.out.println(count);
```

`(int) 19.99` is `19`, not `20`. If you want rounding, call `Math.round` first. Compound assignments such as `+=` and `*=` update a variable in place.

```java predict id=numbers-predict title="What does Java print?"
System.out.println(-7 % 3);
--- options
* -1
1
-2
2
--- explain
In Java the remainder keeps the sign of the left operand. `-7 / 3` is `-2` (truncating toward zero), and `-7 - (-2 * 3)` is `-1`.
```

## Challenges

```java exercise id=average-three title="Average three integers"
int a = 7;
int b = 8;
int c = 10;
double average = 0;

// Compute the average with a decimal result.
--- solution
int a = 7;
int b = 8;
int c = 10;
double average = (a + b + c) / 3.0;
--- test
__check(Math.abs(average - 8.333333333333334) < 0.0001, "The average of 7, 8 and 10 is 8.333...");
--- hint
Add the three values first, then divide by `3.0`.
--- hint
`double average = (a + b + c) / 3.0;`
```

```java exercise id=digit-sum title="Sum the digits of a number"
int number = 472;
int sum = 0;

// Use / and % to add the three digits.
--- solution
int number = 472;
int sum = 0;

sum += number % 10;
number /= 10;
sum += number % 10;
number /= 10;
sum += number % 10;
--- test
__check(sum == 13, "4 + 7 + 2 is 13");
--- hint
`number % 10` gives the last digit. `number / 10` removes it.
--- hint
Repeat the pair three times, once for each digit.
```

```java debug id=fix-integer-average title="The average is missing its decimal"
int a = 3;
int b = 4;
double average = (a + b) / 2;
System.out.println(average);
--- solution
int a = 3;
int b = 4;
double average = (a + b) / 2.0;
System.out.println(average);
--- test
__check(__out().trim().equals("3.5"), "The average of 3 and 4 is 3.5");
--- hint
The division happens before the result is stored in the `double`.
--- hint
Change `2` to `2.0` so the division uses decimal arithmetic.
```
