haracters","`equals` is faster in every case","There is no difference in Java"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`==` asks whether both variables refer to the same object. Two different `String` objects can contain identical text, and only `equals` checks that text."},{"id":"q8","prompt":"What does `System.out.println(\"Sum: \" + (2 + 3))` display?","options":["`Sum: 23`","`Sum: 5`","`Sum: 2 + 3`","`5`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The parentheses force `2 + 3` to be calculated first. The resulting `5` is then joined to the string."},{"id":"q9","prompt":"What happens when `Integer.parseInt(\"hello\")` runs?","options":["It returns `0`","It returns `null`","It throws `NumberFormatException`","It prints a warning and returns `-1`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`parseInt` requires text that represents an integer. `hello` does not, so it throws `NumberFormatException`."},{"id":"q10","prompt":"Why might `long total = 2_000_000_000 * 2;` produce a negative number?","options":["The underscores are invalid in numeric literals","The multiplication happens as `int` before the result is stored in the `long`","`long` cannot hold values above one billion","Java randomly overflows large literals"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Both operands are `int`, so the multiplication overflows in 32 bits. Write `2_000_000_000L * 2` to force 64-bit arithmetic."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_java_basics_3_1', 'sec_java_basics_3', 'crs_java_basics', 'Making decisions with if, else if and else', 'interactive', 0, 1080, NULL, NULL, '`if` runs a block only when a condition is true. `else` runs when it is false. `else if` chains several possibilities, and only the first matching branch runs.

## A single decision

```java run
int temperature = 31;

if (temperature > 30) {
    System.out.println("It is hot.");
}

System.out.println("Done checking.");
```

The braces define the block controlled by the `if`. The final line always runs because it is outside the braces. Indentation is for humans; the braces are what the compiler follows.

## Two branches

```java run
int age = 16;

if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Adult");
} else {
    System.out.println("Minor");
}
```

Exactly one of the two blocks runs. The condition must be a `boolean`; Java will not accept `if (age)` the way some languages do.

## Several branches

```java run
int score = 85;

if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("B");
} else if (score >= 70) {
    System.out.println("C");
} else {
    System.out.println("Needs work");
}
```

Order matters. Once a condition matches, the rest are skipped. If the first check were `score >= 70`, an 85 would get a `C` and the later branches would never be considered.

## The conditional operator

For a short either/or choice, the ternary operator is compact:

```java run
int stock = 0;
String status = stock > 0 ? "In stock" : "Sold out";
System.out.println(status);

int a = 12;
int b = 30;
int larger = a > b ? a : b;
System.out.println(larger);
```

`condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse` produces a value. Use it when both choices are simple; a full `if` is easier to read when the branches contain several statements.

