`","`items.size()`","`items.count`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`ArrayList` is a class, so it exposes `size()` as a method. Arrays use the `length` field."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"For `ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"));`, what does `list.remove(1)` remove?","options":["The value `\"1\"`","The element at index 1, which is `\"b\"`","The first element","Nothing, because `remove` only accepts strings"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An `int` argument selects the overload that removes by index. Index 1 holds `\"b\"`."},{"id":"q8","prompt":"What does `map.get(\"missing\")` return for a `HashMap<String, Integer>`?","options":["`0`","`null`","`-1`","It throws an exception"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A missing key returns `null`. Use `getOrDefault` if you need a numeric fallback."},{"id":"q9","prompt":"What is the size of a `HashSet` after adding `\"a\"`, `\"a\"`, and `\"b\"`?","options":["`1`","`2`","`3`","`0`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A set ignores duplicates. It contains `\"a\"` and `\"b\"`, so its size is `2`."},{"id":"q10","prompt":"What does `Arrays.sort(values)` do to the array?","options":["Returns a new sorted copy and leaves the original alone","Sorts the array in place and returns nothing","Reverses the array","Only works on `String[]`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`Arrays.sort` mutates the array in place. If you need to keep the original order, copy the array first."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_java_basics_5_1', 'sec_java_basics_5', 'crs_java_basics', 'Methods: parameters and return values', 'interactive', 0, 1080, NULL, NULL, 'A **method** is a named block of code that can take inputs and return a result. Methods let you name an idea once and reuse it, instead of copying the same lines into every place that needs them.

> **Note:** Methods are declared inside a class, not inside another method. In this lesson every cell is a complete class, so the editor starts with `public class Main`.

## Declaring and calling

```java run class
public class Main {
    static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int result = add(3, 5);
        System.out.println(result);
        System.out.println(add(10, 20));
    }
}
```

The method header has four parts: the return type `int`, the name `add`, the parameter list `(int a, int b)`, and the body in braces. `return` sends a value back to the caller. `static` means the method belongs to the class itself, so `main` can call it without creating an object.

## Parameters and arguments

```java run class
public class Main {
    static double area(double width, double height) {
        return width * height;
    }

    static String greet(String name) {
        return "Hello, " + name + "!";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(area(3, 4));
        System.out.println(greet("Ada"));
    }
}
```

The names in the declaration (`width`, `height`) are **parameters**. The values at the call site (`3`, `4`) are **arguments**. Java checks that the argument types match the parameter types.

## Methods that return nothing

```java run class
public class Main {
    static void printLine(int width) {
        for (int i = 0; i < width; i++) {
            System.out.print("-");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        printLine(5);
        printLine(12);
    }
}
```

`void` means "returns no value". A `void` method can still have side effects such as printing. You cannot assign its result to a variable.

## Returning early

```java run class
public class Main {
    static String classify(int n) {
        if (n < 0) {
            return "negative";
        }
        if (n == 0) {
            return "zero";
        }
        return "positive";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(classify(-4));
        System.out.println(classify(0));
        System.out.println(classify(9));
    }
}
```

Once a `return` runs, the method ends immediately. The compiler requires every possible path through a non-void method to return a value; that is why the final `return` is outside the `if` blocks.

```java predict class id=methods-predict title="What is printed?"
public class Main {
    static int doubleIt(int n) {
        return n * 2;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(doubleIt(3) + doubleIt(4));
    }
}
--- options
* 14
7
24
12
--- explain
`doubleIt(3)` is `6` and `doubleIt(4)` is `8`. The two results are added, giving `14`.
```

## Challenges

