('les_java_basics_5_5', 'sec_java_basics_5', 'crs_java_basics', 'Checkpoint: methods', 'quiz', 4, 420, NULL, NULL, 'Answer all questions — you need **70%** to pass. You can retake the quiz as many times as you like.', 0, '[{"id":"q1","prompt":"What does a `void` return type mean?","options":["The method returns `null`","The method returns no value","The method cannot take parameters","The method is private"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`void` means the method performs an action but does not send a value back. You cannot assign its result to a variable."},{"id":"q2","prompt":"In `static int add(int a, int b)`, what are `a` and `b` called?","options":["Arguments","Parameters","Return values","Fields"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The names in the method declaration are parameters. The values supplied at the call site, such as `add(2, 3)`, are arguments."},{"id":"q3","prompt":"Why can `main` call a method marked `static` without creating an object?","options":["`static` methods are copied into `main`","`static` members belong to the class itself","`main` is not really a method","Java creates a hidden object automatically"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A static method belongs to the class, not to an instance. `main` is static too, so it can call other static methods directly."},{"id":"q4","prompt":"A non-void method has an `if` with a `return` but no `return` after it. What does the compiler do?","options":["Returns `0` automatically","Returns `null` automatically","Reports that a return value is missing on some path","Runs the `if` branch twice"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Every possible path through a non-void method must return a value. The compiler checks this before the program runs."},{"id":"q5","prompt":"Where can a variable declared inside a `for` loop body be used?","options":["Anywhere in the method","Only inside that loop body block","Only after the loop","In other methods of the same class"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A local variable''s scope is the block where it is declared. It disappears at the closing brace of that block."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"When an `int` is passed to a method and the method changes its parameter, what happens to the caller''s variable?","options":["It changes too","It is unchanged, because Java passes a copy","It becomes `null`","The program does not compile"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Java is pass-by-value. The method receives a copy of the value, so reassigning the parameter does not affect the caller''s variable."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"Which two methods correctly overload each other?","options":["`int area(int s)` and `double area(int s)`","`int area(int s)` and `int area(int w, int h)`","`int area(int s)` and `int area(int side)`","`void area(int s)` and `int area(int s)`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Overloads must differ in their parameter lists. Differing only by return type or parameter names is not enough."},{"id":"q8","prompt":"What is the essential part of every recursive method?","options":["A loop","A base case that stops the recursion","A `static` modifier","An array parameter"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Without a base case, the method keeps calling itself until the stack overflows. Each recursive call must also move closer to that base case."},{"id":"q9","prompt":"What does the parameter `int... numbers` accept?","options":["Exactly one integer","Zero or more integers, packed into an array","Only an `int[]` variable","Any object type"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Varargs accepts a variable number of arguments. Inside the method, `numbers` behaves like an `int[]`."},{"id":"q10","prompt":"What is a guard clause?","options":["A loop that protects an array","An early return for an invalid or edge case","A `try` block around every method","A `static` field used as a lock"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A guard clause handles the invalid case first and returns early, leaving the main path unindented and easier to read."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.17