('les_java_basics_6_4', 'sec_java_basics_6', 'crs_java_basics', 'Checkpoint: errors, files & packages', 'quiz', 3, 420, NULL, NULL, 'Answer all questions — you need **70%** to pass. You can retake the quiz as many times as you like.', 0, '[{"id":"q1","prompt":"What happens when code inside a `try` block throws an exception that matches a `catch`?","options":["The rest of the `try` block still runs","Control jumps to the matching `catch` block","The program always exits","The exception is ignored"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The remaining statements in `try` are skipped. Execution moves to the first matching `catch`, then continues after the try/catch."},{"id":"q2","prompt":"When does a `finally` block run?","options":["Only when the `try` succeeds","Only when an exception is caught","Whether the `try` succeeds, throws, or returns","Only if there is no `catch`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`finally` is for cleanup, so it runs on every path out of the try/catch structure, including an early `return`."},{"id":"q3","prompt":"Which statement about checked exceptions is true?","options":["They never need to be handled","The compiler requires callers to catch or declare them","They only occur in loops","They are the same as `RuntimeException`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A checked exception is part of a method''s contract. The compiler ensures every caller catches it or declares it with `throws`."},{"id":"q4","prompt":"Why must `catch (NumberFormatException e)` come before `catch (Exception e)`?","options":["Java checks catch blocks alphabetically","The broader `Exception` catch would make the specific one unreachable","`NumberFormatException` is not an `Exception`","It makes the program run faster"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A specific subclass must be caught before its superclass. Otherwise the broad catch would handle everything and the compiler rejects the unreachable one."},{"id":"q5","prompt":"What does `throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"bad input\");` do?","options":["Prints the message and continues","Creates an exception and immediately raises it","Returns `\"bad input\"` to the caller","Only works inside a `catch` block"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`throw` raises the exception immediately. It travels up the call stack until a matching `catch` handles it."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"What does `Files.write(path, lines)` do by default?","options":["Appends to the file without changing existing content","Creates or replaces the file and writes the lines","Only writes if the file already exists","Returns the file''s contents"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The default options create the file if needed and truncate an existing file, so the result contains exactly the supplied lines."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"What type does `Files.readAllLines(path)` return?","options":["`String`","`String[]`","`List<String>`","`ArrayList<String>`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"It returns a `List<String>`, one string per line. You can call `size()` or iterate over it."},{"id":"q8","prompt":"Which option adds text to the end of an existing file?","options":["`StandardOpenOption.CREATE`","`StandardOpenOption.TRUNCATE_EXISTING`","`StandardOpenOption.APPEND`","`StandardOpenOption.READ`"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"`APPEND` writes after the existing content. Combine it with `CREATE` if the file may not exist yet."},{"id":"q9","prompt":"Where do `import` statements go?","options":["Inside `main`","After the class declaration","After the package declaration and before the class","At the end of the file"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Imports come after the package statement and before any class declarations. The runtime adds the package line for you."},{"id":"q10","prompt":"Why is no import needed for `System.out.println`?","options":["`System` is a primitive type","`java.lang` is imported automatically","`println` is a keyword","Every class is in the default package"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`System`, `String`, `Math`, `Integer` and the other core classes live in `java.lang`, which every Java file imports implicitly."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_java_basics_7_1', 'sec_java_basics_7', 'crs_java_basics', 'Classes and objects: your own data types', 'interactive', 0, 1200, NULL, NULL, 'A **class** is a blueprint. An **object** is one instance built from that blueprint. Classes bundle data (fields) with the operations that belong to that data (methods), which is the heart of object-oriented programming.

## Fields, constructors and methods

```java run class
class Counter {
    int value;

    Counter(int start) {
        value = start;
    }

    void increment() {
        value++;
    }

    int getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Counter counter = new Counter(10);
        counter.increment();
        counter.increment();
        System.out.println(counter.getValue());
    }
}
```

`new Counter(10)` allocates an object and runs the constructor. The constructor has the same name as the class and no return type. Fields belong to each object, so two counters keep separate values.

## this

```java run class
class Person {
    String name;
    int age;

    Person(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    void birthday() {
        this.age++;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Person ada = new Person("Ada", 36);
        ada.birthday();
        System.out.println(ada.name + " is " + ada.age);
    }
}
```

`this` refers to the current object. It is essential when a parameter has the same name as a field: `this.name = name` assigns the parameter to the field, while `name = name` would assign the parameter to itself.

## Encapsulation

```java run class
class Temperature {
    private double celsius;

    Temperature(double celsius) {
        this.celsius = celsius;
    }

    double getCelsius() {
        return celsius;
    }

    double getFahrenheit() {
        return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;
    }

    void setCelsius(double celsius) {
        if (celsius < -273.15) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Below absolute zero");
        }
        this.celsius = celsius;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Temperature t = new Temperature(25);
        System.out.println(t.getFahrenheit());
        t.setCelsius(30);
        System.out.println(t.getCelsius());
    }
}
```

A `private` field cannot be read or written from outside the class. Access goes through methods, which can validate input. This is **encapsulation**: the object protects its own invariants.

## toString and static members

```java run class
class Point {
    int x;
    int y;

    Point(int x, int y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }

    public String toString() {
        return "(" + x + ", " + y + ")";
    }

    static Point origin() {
        return new Point(0, 0);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Point p = new Point(3, 4);
        System.out.println(p);
        System.out.println(Point.origin());
    }
}
```

`toString` is called automatically when an object is printed. A `static` method belongs to the class, not to a particular object, so you call it with `Point.origin()`.

