('les_java_basics_7_3', 'sec_java_basics_7', 'crs_java_basics', 'Checkpoint: objects & classes', 'quiz', 2, 420, NULL, NULL, 'Answer all questions — you need **70%** to pass. You can retake the quiz as many times as you like.', 0, '[{"id":"q1","prompt":"What is the relationship between a class and an object?","options":["A class is one instance of an object","An object is one instance created from a class blueprint","They are two names for the same thing","An object must contain at least one `main` method"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The class describes the fields and methods. Each object is a separate instance with its own field values."},{"id":"q2","prompt":"Which statement about a constructor is true?","options":["It must return `void`","It has the same name as the class and no return type","It can only be called once per program","It must be `static`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A constructor shares the class name, has no return type, and runs when `new` creates an object."},{"id":"q3","prompt":"Why is `this.name = name;` useful in a constructor?","options":["It creates a second field","It assigns the parameter to the field when both have the same name","It makes the field `static`","It calls the parent constructor"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`this.name` refers to the object''s field, while `name` on the right is the parameter. Without `this`, the assignment would target the parameter."},{"id":"q4","prompt":"What does marking a field `private` do?","options":["Prevents the field from being initialized","Allows access only from inside the same class","Makes the field read-only everywhere","Stores the field in a file"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`private` hides the field from other classes. Code outside the class must use public methods, which can validate values."},{"id":"q5","prompt":"What is `toString()` used for?","options":["Converting a class to a file","Providing a readable string when an object is printed or concatenated","Deleting an object from memory","Comparing two objects for equality"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`println` and string concatenation call `toString` automatically. Overriding it gives objects a useful display form."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"How do you call a `static` method named `origin` on a class named `Point`?","options":["`new Point().origin()`","`Point.origin()`","`Point->origin()`","`origin(Point)`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A static method belongs to the class, so it is called with the class name. An instance is not required."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"Which keyword establishes inheritance?","options":["`implements`","`extends`","`inherits`","`super`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A class inherits from another class with `extends`. `implements` is for interfaces."},{"id":"q8","prompt":"What does overriding a method mean?","options":["Declaring two methods with the same name in one class","Providing a subclass version of a method inherited from the parent","Making a method `static`","Calling the parent method twice"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An override has the same signature as a parent method and replaces its behaviour for the subclass."},{"id":"q9","prompt":"When must a subclass call `super(...)`?","options":["Always, in every constructor","When the parent has no no-argument constructor","Only when the subclass has no constructor","Only for static methods"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Java calls the parent''s no-argument constructor automatically. If the parent only has a constructor with parameters, the subclass must call it explicitly."},{"id":"q10","prompt":"What does this print? `Animal a = new Dog(); System.out.println(a.speak());` where `Dog` overrides `speak()`.","options":["The `Animal` version, because the variable type is `Animal`","The `Dog` version, because the object is a `Dog`","A compile error","`null`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Java dispatches overridden methods based on the object''s runtime type, not the variable''s declared type. The 