('les_java_basics_8_4', 'sec_java_basics_8', 'crs_java_basics', 'Final exam: Java Basics', 'quiz', 3, 420, NULL, NULL, 'Answer all questions — you need **70%** to pass. You can retake the quiz as many times as you like.', 0, '[{"id":"q1","prompt":"A grade report method returns `sum / scores.length` and the average is always a whole number. What is the fix?","options":["Use `Math.round(sum / scores.length)`","Cast before dividing: `(double) sum / scores.length`","Store `sum` in a `String`","Use `%` instead of `/`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Both operands are integers, so the division truncates. Casting `sum` to `double` forces decimal division."},{"id":"q2","prompt":"Why does a `for` loop that removes items from an `ArrayList` often skip elements?","options":["`ArrayList` cannot remove items","Each removal shifts later elements left, so the next index is skipped","The loop variable is always reset","`remove` only works on the last element"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Removing an element closes the gap by shifting later elements left. Iterating backwards, or using an `Iterator`, avoids skipping."},{"id":"q3","prompt":"What is the safest way to read a count from a `HashMap<String, Integer>` when the key may be missing?","options":["`map.get(key) + 1`","`map.getOrDefault(key, 0)`","`map.containsKey(key) + 1`","`map.remove(key)`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"`get` returns `null` for a missing key, and unboxing `null` for arithmetic throws `NullPointerException`. `getOrDefault` supplies a real number."},{"id":"q4","prompt":"What is the main guarantee of a `HashSet`?","options":["Elements stay in insertion order","Elements are sorted automatically","It contains no duplicate values","Every element has an integer key"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A set stores each distinct value once. It does not promise order; use a `TreeSet` for sorted order or a `LinkedHashSet` for insertion order."},{"id":"q5","prompt":"What happens if a recursive method has no base case?","options":["It returns `null`","It runs until the JVM throws `StackOverflowError`","It automatically becomes a loop","It compiles but never runs"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Each call adds a frame to the call stack. Without a base case the stack eventually runs out of space."},{"id":"q6","prompt":"In a `try / catch / finally` block, which parts run when the `try` throws a caught exception?","options":["Only the `try`","The `catch` and then the `finally`","Only the `finally`","The `try` again, then the `finally`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The matching `catch` handles the exception, and `finally` runs afterwards on the way out."},{"id":"q7","prompt":"Why does a bank account keep its `balance` field `private`?","options":["Private fields use less memory","So changes go through methods that can enforce the account rules","So the field cannot be printed","Because `double` fields must be private"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Encapsulation protects the invariant that the balance never goes negative. A public field could be changed to any value by any code."},{"id":"q8","prompt":"`Animal a = new Dog();` where `Dog` extends `Animal` and overrides `speak()`. Which `speak()` runs?","options":["`Animal.speak()`","`Dog.speak()`","Both, one after the other","Neither; the call is ambiguous"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The declared type controls what you can call, but the runtime object controls which override executes. The `Dog` version runs."},{"id":"q9","prompt":"Why use a `StringBuilder` instead of joining many pieces with `+` in a loop?","options":["`StringBuilder` can store numbers without conversion","Strings are immutable, so each `+` creates a new string","`+` does not work inside loops","`StringBuilder` sorts the pieces"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Because strings cannot change, repeated concatenation allocates a new string each time. `StringBuilder` appends into one growing buffer."},{"id":"q10","prompt":"What does this print? `for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { System.out.print(i); }`","options":["`123`","`012`","`0123`","`0 1 2`"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The loop runs for 0, 1 and 2. `print` keeps them on one line, producing `012`."}]', '[]', '2026-06-25T06:40:00.179Z', '2026-10-19T06:40:00.179Z'),
('les_c_basics_1_1', 'sec_c_basics_1', 'crs_c_basics', 'How this course works and setting up a C compiler', 'interactive', 0, 600, NULL, NULL, 'C is the language that most of the software you use every day is built on. The kernel that runs your phone, the database behind your favourite app, the firmware in your microwave: many of them are written in C, because C gives you direct control over memory and the machine underneath. It is also a language that rewards care. It will not hold your hand, and that is exactly why people who learn it well understand computers deeply.

This course takes you from a blank file to small real programs. You will learn the syntax, the types, the memory model and the habits that experienced C programmers rely on.

## How this course works

Each lesson mixes three things:

- **Short explanations.** We build intuition first, then state the precise rule.
- **Complete programs.** Every example is a whole C file that compiles on its own, not a fragment you have to guess about.
- **Predict-the-output questions.** Before the answer is revealed, you choose what the program prints. Each question has an explanation that teaches the rule behind the answer. Treat these as the heart of the course. If you get one wrong, you have found something worth understanding.

Here is a question you will see in the next lesson, so you know what to expect:

```c predict id=preview-question title="What does this print?"
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    printf("C");
    printf("!\n");
    return 0;
}
--- options
C !
* C!
C
!C
--- explain
Each `printf` writes exactly what you tell it to and nothing more. There is no automatic space and no automatic newline, so the two calls join into one line: `C!` followed by a newline.
```

> **Tip:** Answer before you reveal. The attempt is where the learning happens, even when you are wrong.

## Get a C compiler

C is compiled. You write source code in a text file, a compiler turns it into a program, and then you run that program. Install one of the following, then check it works.

**Windows.** Install MinGW-w64 or use the Microsoft Visual Studio Build Tools with `cl`. Most learners find it easiest to install **MSYS2** and then run `pacman -S mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gcc` from its terminal.

**macOS.** Run `xcode-select --install`. It installs `clang`, which is fully compatible with the examples here.

**Linux.** Install gcc with your package manager, for example `sudo apt install build-essential` on Debian and Ubuntu.

Confirm the installation from a terminal:

```bash
gcc --version
```

Every run-it-yourself step in this course uses this command pattern, where `hello.c` is your source file:

```bash
gcc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic hello.c -o hello
./hello
```

The flags are worth understanding from day one. `-std=c11` selects the standard, `-Wall -Wextra` asks the compiler to warn about suspicious code, and `-pedantic` keeps you to the standard. On Windows the program is called `hello.exe`, and you run it with `hello` or `.\hello`.

> **Pitfall:** Treat every warning as an error to fix, not noise to ignore. Many of the bugs in this course, such as using a variable before it has a value, are warnings first.

## What you will be able to do

By the end of the course you will write programs that read input, make decisions, loop over data, use functions, store collections in arrays and strings, follow pointers through memory, and save records to a file. Every concept comes with a predict question so you can check your reasoning before you compile.

## Recap

- C is compiled. A compiler such as `gcc` or `clang` turns your source file into a program you run.
- Install a compiler once, then use the same `gcc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic` command for every program.
- Predict-the-output questions are the core of each lesson. Answer first, then read the explanation.
- Read compiler warnings as signals, not noise.
- Every example is a complete program, so you can always run exactly what you see.

**Up next:** your first program, and how `printf` turns a line of code into output.
